var tafsirData_15 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with three disconnected letters, as several chapters do, and the position held by many early scholars is that their complete meaning rests with Allah. A frequently made observation is that these are ordinary letters of the Arabic alphabet, the same ones used in the poetry the Arabs prized, placed at the head of a Book they were invited to match and could not.\n\n\"These are the verses of the Book; the clear Quran.\" names what follows with two descriptions, and they work together.\n\nCalling it the Book indicates something written, fixed, and preserved rather than a collection of sayings. The definite form suggests the Book that had been expected, the one earlier scriptures pointed toward.\n\nThe word \"Quran\" comes from a root meaning to recite or read aloud, which describes how it was actually received and how it has been transmitted ever since. The Book is written and it is also recited, and these two aspects have preserved it in parallel for fourteen centuries: copies on paper and complete memorisation in the hearts of people in every generation.\n\n\"Clear\" translates an Arabic word indicating something that is itself plain and that also makes other things plain. Both senses apply. The Quran states its message without obscurity, and it clarifies matters human beings could never resolve on their own.\n\nThat description sets the tone for a chapter that will deal with people who found the message perfectly clear and rejected it anyway. Within a few verses they are quoted calling the Prophet insane and demanding that angels be sent down.\n\nThe opening therefore does something quiet but important. Before any of their objections are recorded, the reader is told what they were actually objecting to: a Book that is clear. Whatever their complaints amounted to, confusion was not among them.",
  "2": "\"˹The day will come when˺ the disbelievers will certainly wish they had submitted ˹to Allah˺.\" is the first statement in the chapter after its opening, and it looks forward to a moment of regret.\n\nThe Arabic construction indicates something that will happen repeatedly, often understood as a wish that recurs whenever they see the consequences of their choice. Commentators mentioned that this wish comes at various points: at death, in the grave, at the reckoning, and above all when believers are admitted to Paradise while they are not.\n\nThere is a report explained by some early scholars that when sinful believers are eventually removed from the Fire through Allah's mercy, those who remain wish most bitterly that they had been Muslims.\n\nThe word for what they will wish they had been indicates those who submit, who surrender themselves to Allah. This is the definition of the word Muslim, and the Quran applies it to the followers of every prophet.\n\nThe verse repays reflection because of what it says about desire. These were people who believed they were choosing freedom by refusing, and who treated submission as a loss of independence. The verse states that the same people will one day want nothing more than the thing they refused.\n\nThat reversal is worth noticing while there is still time to act on it. A person can examine his own resistance to a religious obligation and ask a simple question: will I regret this later? Most reluctance in matters of worship comes from valuing the present far above the future, which is exactly the miscalculation this verse identifies.\n\nPlacing this at the very start of the chapter is deliberate. Before any argument is presented, the outcome is announced, which frames everything that follows as a warning delivered while the wish can still be acted on.",
  "3": "\"˹So˺ let them eat and enjoy themselves and be diverted by ˹false˺ hope\" is not permission, and reading it as such would miss its force entirely.\n\nThe Arabic imperative here functions as a warning delivered with heavy irony, telling them to carry on with what they are doing since the outcome is already known. The same form appeared at the end of Surah Ibrahim, where the Prophet was told to say enjoy yourselves and that the destination is the Fire.\n\nThree activities are named, and together they describe a complete way of life.\n\nEating stands for physical appetite, the pursuit of what the body wants. Enjoyment covers pleasure more broadly. Neither is condemned in itself, and Islam does not treat food and enjoyment as evils.\n\nThe third item is the one that turns a normal life into a wasted one. The Arabic word \"amal\" indicates hope, expectation, the long chain of plans and assumptions a person carries about the future. Being diverted by it means letting those expectations occupy the mind so completely that nothing else registers.\n\nThis is the mechanism by which most people avoid thinking about death. Not by arguing against it, but by always having something ahead: a project to finish, a stage of life to reach, a plan that will make everything settle. The Prophet drew a picture of exactly this, described in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, drawing lines to show a man's hopes stretching beyond the point where his lifespan ends.\n\n\"For they will soon know.\" closes the verse, and the word for soon is doing quiet work.\n\nThe chapter had already placed a one-line preview of that knowledge in the verse before this one, saying the disbelievers will certainly wish they had submitted. The hoping that occupies them now and the wishing that awaits them are opposites: one pushes the reckoning out of mind, the other would trade everything to undo what was pushed aside.\n\nFrom a human standpoint, the reckoning seems distant. From the standpoint of the One speaking, it is imminent. Both are true, and the difference between them is exactly the gap that false hope fills.",
  "4": "\"We have never destroyed a society without a destined term.\" answers a question raised by the warning in the previous verses.\n\nIf people are being told they will soon know, why has nothing happened yet? The answer is that destruction, when it comes, arrives at an appointed time that was fixed in advance.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates a known, written decree. Nothing in the sequence is improvised. A community's period of grace is not extended because Allah is hesitating, and it does not end because His patience runs out.\n\nThe verse also makes a point about how divine punishment has always operated. It says we have never destroyed a society without such a term, which means every case recorded in the Quran followed this pattern. Noah's people were given generations. Thamud were given three days after killing the camel. Pharaoh was given plague after plague with opportunities to relent between each one.\n\nThere is something in this for understanding history more generally. Civilisations do not collapse the moment they become corrupt. There is usually a long period in which the decay is visible and the structure still stands, and people inside that period conclude that nothing is wrong precisely because nothing has happened yet.\n\nThe Prophet's audience in Mecca was living inside such a period, and they treated the delay as their argument. Surah Hud recorded them asking what was holding the punishment back.\n\nUnderstanding the appointed term changes how a believer reads both his own situation and the world around him. Injustice continuing does not mean it is permitted, and a warning unfulfilled does not mean it was empty.\n\nIt also removes the temptation to predict. Since the term is known only to Allah, nobody can announce when a community's time has come, and claims of that kind have always been the mark of people speaking without knowledge.",
  "5": "\"No people can advance their doom, nor can they delay it.\" completes the principle stated in the previous verse, and it closes both directions at once.\n\nThe Arabic wording covers the whole possibility. A community cannot bring its appointed end forward, and it cannot push it back. Whatever it does, the timing holds.\n\nThis exact formulation appears in several places in the Quran. Surah al-A'raf states it about every community having an appointed term, and Surah Yunus repeats it in the context of people demanding to know when the threatened punishment would arrive.\n\nThe repetition across chapters is worth noticing, because it addresses two different kinds of people with the same sentence.\n\nThe first are those who mock, demanding that the punishment be hurried along as proof. Surah Yunus recorded them doing exactly this, and asked whether the wicked realise what they are asking to be hastened. This verse tells them their demand changes nothing.\n\nThe second are those who assume they can postpone consequences indefinitely through wealth, alliances, or precautions. That is a more common attitude, and it operates quietly in prosperous societies. Preparations are made, defences are built, and the assumption grows that the arrangement can be sustained. The verse says the term does not move.\n\nThe Arabic word used here indicates the appointed time itself, and its use for both individuals and communities is deliberate. What applies to a nation applies to a person. Nobody dies early and nobody dies late, whatever the immediate cause appears to be.\n\nThere is a settling effect in believing this properly. Fear of death produces a great deal of anxiety, much of it directed at avoiding what cannot be avoided. Accepting that the moment is fixed does not remove the instinct to be careful, since taking precautions is commanded, but it removes the illusion that carefulness is what keeps a person alive.",
  "6": "\"They say, \"O you to whom the Reminder is revealed! You must be insane!\"\" records an accusation with a strange feature built into it.\n\nNotice how they address him. They call him the one to whom the Reminder is revealed, and then immediately declare him mad. The two halves of the sentence contradict each other, and commentators have pointed this out. Most understood the opening phrase as sarcasm, mocking his claim rather than conceding it.\n\nEither way the accusation itself was their standard weapon. The Arabic word indicates possession by jinn, being touched in the mind, which was the common explanation offered for anyone whose behaviour departed from expectation.\n\nThe charge was convenient rather than considered. If the man is unwell, his message requires no answer. Nobody debates a madman, and dismissing him costs nothing.\n\nWhat makes the accusation so weak is the character of the person it was aimed at. The Meccans had known him for forty years before he began preaching. They called him the trustworthy one, deposited their valuables with him, and asked him to settle their disputes. Nothing in his conduct, speech, or judgement suggested instability, and the Quran responds elsewhere by pointing out that their companion is not mad, and that by the favour of his Lord he is not possessed.\n\nThis accusation was not new to him. Surah adh-Dhariyat observes that no messenger came to those before them without their saying he was a magician or a madman, and asks whether they had all instructed one another to say it.\n\nThere is a pattern here that outlasts the seventh century. When a message cannot be answered, the messenger's competence becomes the topic. It is easier to question someone's sanity, motives, or stability than to engage with what he actually said, and the effect is the same: the conversation ends without anyone having to think.",
  "7": "\"\"Why do you not bring us the angels, if what you say is true?\"\" is the demand that follows their accusation, and it reveals what they thought would settle the matter.\n\nThey wanted visible angels. Not an argument, not a scripture, but beings descending in front of them, confirming the Prophet's claim beyond any possibility of doubt.\n\nThe demand appears repeatedly in the Quran. Surah al-Furqan records them asking why an angel was not sent down to him to be a warner alongside him, and Surah al-An'am notes that if an angel had been sent, the matter would have been decided and they would have been given no respite.\n\nThat last point is the crucial one, and the next verse states it directly. Angels arriving in visible form is not a neutral demonstration. In the accounts recorded throughout the Quran, when angels come openly to a rejecting people, they come to carry out the sentence. Lot's visitors arrived as guests and left as the instrument of a city's destruction.\n\nSo the request was more dangerous than the people making it understood. They were asking for the one event that would end the period of grace the chapter has just described.\n\nThere is also a deeper problem with the demand, and the following verses expose it. People who refuse a message do not usually refuse it for lack of evidence, and the chapter goes on to say that even if a gate to heaven were opened and they climbed through it, they would claim their eyes had been tricked.\n\nThe condition attached, \"if what you say is true,\" has the tone of a challenge rather than an inquiry. It is the same phrase the peoples of Noah and Hud used when daring their prophets to bring on the promised punishment.\n\nA person genuinely investigating a claim examines the evidence available. A person setting terms for what would convince him has usually decided already.",
  "8": "\"We do not send the angels down except for a just cause\" answers the demand of the previous verse, and it corrects the assumption behind it.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that angels descend only with the truth, meaning with a decisive matter: revelation delivered to a prophet, or the execution of a decree. They are not sent to satisfy curiosity or to perform for an audience that has set conditions.\n\nThis is worth understanding properly, because the demand for angels sounds reasonable on its surface. Why not simply provide the evidence people ask for?\n\nThe answer lies in what such an appearance would mean. Throughout the Quran, when angels appear openly to a community that has rejected its messenger, they arrive to carry out judgement. The angels who visited Abraham with good news continued on to Lot's people, and the city was overturned that morning.\n\n\"And then ˹the end of˺ the disbelievers will not be delayed.\" states this outcome plainly. Once that door opens, the appointed term the chapter described a few verses earlier expires immediately.\n\nSo the demand, if granted, would have destroyed the people making it. What they took for a fair test was in fact a request to have their period of grace cancelled.\n\nThere is a mercy in the refusal that they could not perceive. Many of those same Meccans later became believers. Umar ibn al-Khattab, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Amr ibn al-As all opposed the Prophet fiercely before accepting Islam, and none of them would have had the opportunity if their earlier demand had been met.\n\nThis is a recurring feature of how the Quran presents unanswered requests. What people ask for is not always what would benefit them, and a refusal can be a form of protection. The delay they mocked was the space in which some of them eventually changed.",
  "9": "\"It is certainly We Who have revealed the Reminder, and it is certainly We Who will preserve it.\" is one of the most consequential statements in the Quran, and its two halves belong together.\n\nThe first half answers the accusations recorded a few verses earlier. They had called the Prophet insane and demanded angels. The reply identifies the source of the message: it was sent down by Allah, not produced by the man delivering it.\n\nThe Arabic word \"dhikr,\" rendered here as the Reminder, is used throughout the Quran as a name for the revelation itself. It indicates something that reminds, and the choice of name reflects what the Book does: it recalls to people what heedlessness had buried.\n\nThe second half is the promise. Allah undertakes to preserve it Himself.\n\nThis distinguishes the Quran from earlier scriptures in a way the Quran itself notes. Surah al-Ma'idah mentions that earlier communities were entrusted with the preservation of what was given to them, and describes some of them altering words from their places. Here the responsibility is not delegated.\n\nThe historical outcome is open to examination. The Quran was memorised in full by companions during the Prophet's lifetime, written down as it was revealed, compiled into a single volume under Abu Bakr, and standardised under Uthman. Since then it has been transmitted through two parallel channels: written copies and complete memorisation. Millions of people alive today have the entire text in memory, and a child in Nigeria recites the same words as a child in Indonesia.\n\nNo other book of comparable age has anything resembling this. That is not offered as proof to someone who rejects the claim, but it is a fact that anyone can verify.\n\nFor believers, the promise carries a responsibility rather than an excuse for passivity. Allah preserves it through people, and those who memorise, teach, and transmit it are the means by which the promise operates.",
  "10": "\"Indeed, We sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ among the groups of early peoples\" turns from the promise of preservation to the consolation of history.\n\nThe Arabic word for these groups indicates factions, parties, the communities into which earlier humanity divided. Messengers were sent among them, which means the Prophet's situation was not unprecedented.\n\nThis form of consolation appears constantly in the Meccan chapters, and its method is worth noticing. He is not told that his difficulties will end soon, and he is given no timetable. He is shown that what he is experiencing is the standard experience of the role.\n\nThere is real relief in that for anyone in a difficult position. Much of the weight of hardship comes from the suspicion that something has gone wrong, that this was not supposed to happen. Discovering that the difficulty is normal for the work does not remove it, but it removes the additional burden of confusion.\n\nThe mention of early peoples also establishes continuity. The Prophet was not founding a new religion but continuing a line, and the chapters before this one had recounted that line in detail: Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, Shu'ayb, Abraham, Joseph, and Moses.\n\nFor the Meccans, the same statement carried a different implication. They treated the Prophet's claim as an innovation, something strange appearing in their city. The verse tells them that what is happening among them has happened repeatedly, and the chapters preceding this one had already shown how those episodes ended.\n\nThe verse also prepares the ground for what follows. Having established that messengers were sent, the next verse states the reception they received, and it is the same reception the Prophet was getting in the streets of Mecca.\n\nPlaced together, the two verses form a single argument: this has all happened before, including the part that hurts.",
  "11": "\"But no messenger ever came to them without being mocked.\" completes the statement begun in the previous verse, and the word \"no\" is absolute.\n\nNot most messengers, not many of them, but every single one. There is no exception in the record, which means mockery is not a sign that something has gone wrong with a mission. It is part of the standard experience.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates ridiculing someone, treating him as an object of amusement.\n\nThe chapters just before this one supplied the evidence in detail. Noah was laughed at while building his ship, and told his people that if they mocked him then he would mock them in turn. Hud's people said one of their gods had struck him with madness. Salih's people expressed disappointment that a promising man had gone astray. Shu'ayb was sarcastically called tolerant and sensible. Moses was called a magician.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad received the same treatment in the same words. He had just been called insane a few verses earlier in this chapter.\n\nThere is a reason mockery is so consistently the weapon of choice, and it is worth naming. Ridicule requires no argument, no evidence, and no risk. It ends a conversation without anyone having to think, and it allows an audience to feel superior while remaining ignorant. Against a message that cannot be refuted, it is the most efficient available response.\n\nUnderstanding this changes how a believer receives it. Being laughed at for practising one's faith is not evidence of being wrong, and it is not a sign of failure in how the matter was presented.\n\nThere is also a warning implied for anyone who finds mockery easy. The Quran records this behaviour as characteristic of the people who were eventually destroyed, and Surah al-An'am notes that those who ridiculed were surrounded by the very thing they had ridiculed.",
  "12": "\"This is how We allow disbelief ˹to steep˺ into the hearts of the wicked.\" describes what happens inside people who respond to messengers with mockery.\n\nThe Arabic indicates inserting something into the heart, threading it through so that it passes into the interior. Commentators explained it as disbelief entering and settling in a heart that has made itself receptive to it.\n\nThe verse needs careful handling, because it can be misread as saying that Allah forces people into disbelief and then punishes them for it. The Quran rules that reading out repeatedly. Surah Yunus stated plainly that Allah does not wrong people in the least but that people wrong themselves, and the chapters before this described hearts being sealed only after persistent, knowing refusal.\n\nThe opening phrase, \"this is how,\" points backward to the previous verse. It follows the mockery. Their ridicule of messenger after messenger produced a condition, and the condition was then confirmed.\n\nThere is a pattern in this that anyone can observe in ordinary life. A person who repeatedly dismisses something eventually becomes incapable of taking it seriously. The first refusal requires an effort of will; the hundredth requires nothing at all, because the response has become automatic.\n\nThe description of those affected as wicked is significant. The Arabic term indicates criminals, people who cut themselves off from what is right through deliberate action. The verse is not describing the honestly confused or those who never received a clear message.\n\nWhat this means practically is that mockery is more dangerous to the mocker than to his target. It feels like a position of strength, and it is a process by which a heart closes.\n\nThe following verses show the result: people who would not believe despite the examples of destroyed nations, and who would explain away a gate opened in the sky.",
  "13": "\"They would not believe in this ˹Quran˺ despite the ˹many˺ examples of those ˹destroyed˺ before.\" states the outcome of the condition described in the previous verse.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that the way of earlier peoples has already passed, meaning the precedent is established and known. What happened to those communities is not a secret or a matter of speculation.\n\nThe Meccans had access to this information in several forms. The Quran had recounted the accounts of Noah, Ad, Thamud, Lot, and Midian at length in the chapters immediately preceding this one. They also had physical evidence, since their trade routes passed the ruins of Thamud in the north and the region associated with Lot's cities.\n\nSo the refusal described here is not a refusal made in ignorance. It happens with the examples fully available, which is what makes it so striking.\n\nThere is something worth reflecting on about why examples fail to persuade. History provides the clearest evidence available about consequences, and it is almost universally ignored. Every generation watches the previous one make mistakes and repeats them, often with complete confidence that its own situation is different.\n\nThe reason is usually not a lack of information but a sense of exemption. People accept that a pattern exists and assume they stand outside it. The Meccans knew what happened to Thamud and treated it as ancient history involving other people.\n\nSurah Yusuf made a similar observation, noting how many signs in the heavens and the earth people pass by with indifference.\n\nWhat follows in the next two verses pushes this to its limit. If examples from history do not work, what about direct experience? The chapter answers that question with an image so extreme that it settles the matter entirely, and it explains why the demand for angels a few verses earlier was never a genuine request for evidence.",
  "14": "\"And even if We opened for them a gate to heaven, through which they continued to ascend,\" begins a scenario designed to answer the demand made earlier in this chapter.\n\nThey had asked why angels were not sent down to them. The verse now imagines something considerably greater. Not angels descending, but a gate opened in the sky and the people themselves climbing through it.\n\nThe Arabic indicates continuous ascent, going up steadily. This is not a glimpse or a brief vision. They keep climbing, with the opening above them and the earth receding below.\n\nConsider what such an experience would involve. It would be sustained, physical, and shared by everyone present. There would be no question of a trick of the light or a moment of confusion, since the ascent continues and can be examined at leisure by many people at once.\n\nBy any reasonable standard, this is a demonstration beyond anything they had requested. It exceeds their own terms.\n\nThe verse is constructed this way deliberately, because it is building toward a conclusion about what evidence can and cannot achieve. The Quran has already stated in Surah al-An'am that even if angels had been sent down to them and the dead had spoken to them, they still would not have believed unless Allah willed.\n\nWhat makes this particular image so effective is that it removes every possible objection in advance. There is no ambiguity in it, no room for interpretation, and no dependence on trusting a messenger's report.\n\nThe scenario also exposes an assumption hidden inside their demand. They spoke as though belief were a passive event that evidence simply causes, the way heat melts wax. The Quran teaches otherwise. The heart is a participant, not a spectator, and what a person loves, fears, and protects shapes what he is able to see; evidence enters a heart that is already occupied.\n\nTheir response, given in the next verse, is therefore not an argument about evidence at all. It is a demonstration of something else entirely, and it explains why the chapter said a few verses earlier that disbelief had been threaded into their hearts.",
  "15": "\"\"Our eyes have truly been dazzled! In fact, we must have been bewitched.\" is the response the previous verse was building toward, and it is devastating.\n\nHaving ascended through an opened gate in the sky, they would conclude that their vision had been affected. The Arabic indicates eyes being sealed, blocked, or intoxicated so that they no longer report reality accurately.\n\nTheir second explanation goes further. They would decide they had been placed under a spell. This is the same accusation levelled at Moses in Pharaoh's court and at the Prophet in Mecca, now applied to their own senses.\n\nWhat the verse exposes is the nature of their refusal. It was never about insufficient evidence. A person who explains away a sustained physical experience shared by a crowd will explain away anything, because the explaining is the point.\n\nThis is worth stating carefully, because it is easy to turn into an accusation against anyone who asks questions. The Quran repeatedly invites people to look, examine, travel, and reason. Honest questions are welcomed throughout these chapters. What is being described here is different: a settled determination to reject, dressed in the language of scepticism.\n\nThere is a recognisable form of this in ordinary experience. When someone has decided what he wants to believe, contrary evidence does not change his position; it changes his theory about the evidence. The data was flawed, the witness was unreliable, the demonstration was staged.\n\nNotice also what their explanation costs them. To maintain their position they must abandon trust in their own eyes, which is a serious price. A person who declares his senses unreliable has undermined every other conclusion he has ever reached, including his confidence in his own judgement about the Prophet.\n\nThis is where refusal eventually leads, and the chapter has already explained how a heart arrives there.",
  "16": "\"Indeed, We have placed constellations in the sky, and adorned it for all to see.\" turns from the people who would explain away an opened heaven to the heaven itself, which stands above them every night without being explained away.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates great structures or stations in the sky, and commentators explained it as the major star groupings and the paths through which the sun and moon move across the year. The Arabs navigated by these, timed their travel by them, and knew them by name.\n\nTwo things are being said about the sky at once, and both matter.\n\nThe first is order. Constellations are fixed arrangements that return predictably, which is what makes navigation possible. A traveller crossing a featureless desert at night could find his direction from them, and Surah an-Nahl notes that people are guided by the stars.\n\nThe second is beauty. The sky is described as adorned, made attractive for those who look at it. This is not a functional statement but an aesthetic one, and the Quran mentions it deliberately.\n\nThat combination is worth pausing over. The sky did not have to be beautiful in order to work. Stars could have provided navigation while being ugly or unremarkable. The fact that the arrangement is both useful and beautiful says something about the One who arranged it, and the Quran often draws attention to this pairing in creation.\n\nThere is also a quiet reproach in the verse's placement. The people in the preceding verses demanded a sign in the sky and said they would dismiss it as a trick. Meanwhile a sign has been above them their entire lives, visible every clear night, and they had stopped looking at it.\n\nSurah Yusuf made this exact point, noting how many signs in the heavens and the earth people pass by with indifference. Familiarity, not lack of evidence, is the obstacle.",
  "17": "\"And We protected it from every accursed devil,\" adds a second fact about the sky, and it moves from what can be seen to what cannot.\n\nThe Arabic word for the protection indicates guarding something, keeping it safe from intrusion. The sky is described as secured.\n\nThose excluded are described with a word indicating one who is driven away, stoned, expelled from mercy. It is the same description attached to Satan in the phrase Muslims use when seeking refuge from him before recitation.\n\nThis verse and the one that follows address something the Meccans took seriously. Soothsayers in Arabia claimed access to hidden knowledge, and their claims carried real authority in a society without other means of predicting events. Some of them attributed their information to jinn who brought them news from above.\n\nThe Quran does not simply dismiss this world as imagination. It describes the arrangement accurately and explains its limits, which is a more useful correction than denial would be.\n\nThe relevance to the chapter's argument is direct. The Prophet had been accused of being insane and, elsewhere, of receiving his message the way soothsayers received theirs. This passage draws a clear line between revelation, which comes down protected and complete, and the fragments that were claimed by those who traded in hidden knowledge.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara addresses this explicitly, stating that the devils did not bring the Quran down, that it is not fitting for them, and that they are not able to do so.\n\nThe protection described here therefore serves the promise made a few verses earlier in this chapter. Allah said He would preserve the Reminder, and part of that preservation concerns the channel through which it descends.\n\nWhat happens to any devil who attempts to listen is described in the next verse, using an image the Arabs could observe with their own eyes.",
  "18": "\"Except the one eavesdropping, who is then pursued by a visible flare.\" completes the description of the guarded sky, and it gives the exception to the protection just mentioned.\n\nThe Arabic indicates one who steals a hearing, snatching a fragment by listening in. This is not access; it is theft attempted at the edge, and it is immediately answered.\n\nThe pursuit comes as a clear, piercing flame. The Arabic describes a burning brand or flare that is unmistakable, which connects the passage to something the Arabs observed regularly: the streaks of light that cross the night sky.\n\nThe Prophet explained this matter in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari. He described how the angels speak of a matter decreed in the heavens, and how eavesdroppers snatch a word and pass it down, sometimes being struck before they can deliver it and sometimes conveying it. The fragment then reaches a soothsayer, who mixes it with a hundred lies, and when part of it turns out to be true, people say that he told them a thing that came to pass.\n\nThat explanation clarifies a great deal about how such claims worked. Occasional accuracy in a stream of invention is enough to sustain a reputation, and people remember the one prediction that landed while forgetting the hundred that did not.\n\nIt also draws the distinction the chapter needs. Revelation comes down complete and protected, as the earlier verse promised. What the soothsayers had was a stolen fragment wrapped in fabrication, and the difference between the two is the difference between a scripture and a rumour.\n\nThere is a practical application that has not aged. Claims to hidden knowledge remain a profitable business, and the method has not changed: a few accurate details, a great deal of vagueness, and an audience that remembers the hits. Islam closes this door firmly, and the Prophet warned that whoever visits a fortune-teller and believes him has rejected what was revealed.",
  "19": "\"As for the earth, We spread it out\" brings the argument down from the guarded sky to the ground people walk on.\n\nThe Arabic indicates extending something, stretching it out so that it becomes broad and usable. This describes the earth as it functions for those living on it, which is exactly what a person experiences: a surface laid out for habitation. A very large sphere is, for its inhabitants, spread out, and the Quran uses similar expressions elsewhere describing the earth as a resting place and a bed.\n\n\"And placed upon it firm mountains\" names the next feature. The Arabic word indicates immovable, anchoring masses. Modern geology describes mountains as having deep roots extending far below the surface, and the Quran elsewhere calls them pegs.\n\n\"And caused everything to grow there in perfect balance.\" completes the verse with a phrase worth examining closely.\n\nThe Arabic indicates growing something of every weighed thing, measured out precisely. Two ideas are contained in it. Everything that grows does so in determined quantities, and everything that grows does so in a proportion that fits.\n\nThat proportion is one of the most striking features of the natural world. The plants that grow produce exactly what animals need to breathe, and the animals produce what plants need in return. Soil composition, rainfall, temperature, and growing seasons combine in arrangements that support life rather than destroying it.\n\nThe word for weighing is significant in a Book that repeatedly connects the balance in creation with the balance humans are commanded to observe. Surah ar-Rahman places the raising of the sky and the setting of the balance side by side, and immediately instructs people not to transgress in the balance.\n\nThere is an argument running through these three verses that becomes clear when they are read together. The sky is ordered and protected; the earth is spread out and measured. Nothing about the arrangement suggests accident, and the people demanding an extraordinary sign were standing on one.",
  "20": "\"And We made in it means of sustenance for you\" continues the description of the earth, and it moves from how the ground is arranged to what it produces.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates the means of living, everything a person needs to sustain himself. This includes food and water, and it also covers the wider provision that makes settled life possible: materials, fuel, and the resources that human work depends on.\n\nPlacing this immediately after the mention of balanced growth is deliberate. The earth is not merely stable and orderly; it is stocked. Someone arriving on it finds what he needs already present.\n\n\"And others, who you do not provide for.\" is the phrase that transforms the verse, and it deserves careful reading.\n\nCommentators have understood these others in several complementary ways. Some took it to mean animals, birds, and livestock, whose sustenance grows on the same earth without any human arrangement. Others included servants, dependants, and family members whom a person imagines he supports but whose provision was actually written before he was involved. Some included every creature in existence beyond human awareness.\n\nAll of these readings carry the same point, and it is a humbling one. A person naturally sees himself as the provider for those under his care. He works, earns, and distributes, and it feels like a chain running from his effort to their food.\n\nThe verse quietly cuts that chain. He is not their provider. He is a means through which provision reaches them, and the provision was arranged by someone else.\n\nThis connects to what Surah Hud said about every moving creature on earth having its sustenance guaranteed by Allah. The insects in the soil, the birds overhead, and the creatures in the depths of the sea are all fed on the same earth, and nobody organises it.\n\nFor a person anxious about supporting a household, the reframing is worth absorbing.",
  "21": "\"There is not any means ˹of sustenance˺ whose reserves We do not hold\" makes a claim about everything that exists, and the image behind it is one of storehouses.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates treasuries, places where things are kept in store. Every kind of thing has its reserves with Allah, held and controlled.\n\nThis applies to rain, to crops, to minerals, to knowledge, to health, and to everything else a person might need. The verse says the source of each is not the visible mechanism but a reserve held elsewhere.\n\nThere is something clarifying in this for how a believer views the causes he sees around him. Rain comes from clouds, and clouds come from evaporation, and evaporation comes from heat. All of that is true and worth understanding. What the verse adds is that the whole chain is a means of distribution rather than the origin of the supply.\n\n\"Only bringing it forth in precise measure.\" states how the reserves are released.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a known, determined measure. Nothing is sent down randomly or in unlimited quantity.\n\nThat measurement is one of the conditions of life. Rain in the right amount produces crops, and the same rain multiplied becomes a flood that destroys them. Sunlight in measure allows growth, and beyond it burns. The Quran mentions elsewhere that Allah sends down water in due measure, and that if He expanded provision for His servants without limit they would transgress on the earth.\n\nThat last point is worth dwelling on. Restriction is often experienced as deprivation, and the Quran presents it as part of the arrangement rather than a failure of generosity. A person given everything he wanted immediately would be destroyed by it, and most people can verify this from their own history.\n\nWhat this verse offers, read alongside the ones before it, is a settled way of understanding provision: the reserves are full, the release is measured, and neither is in human hands.",
  "22": "\"We send fertilizing winds\" names something the Arabs knew from experience without knowing its mechanism.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates winds that impregnate or fertilise. Commentators explained this as winds that carry water vapour and drive clouds together until rain falls, and they also noted the role of wind in carrying pollen from plant to plant.\n\nBoth processes are real, and both were understood only in outline by anyone at the time. Wind carries pollen between male and female parts of plants, which is essential for fruit to form. Wind also drives moisture-laden air and pushes cloud masses together, which is part of how rain is produced. The single Arabic word covers both, and the choice is remarkably apt for a description given in seventh-century Arabia.\n\n\"And bring down rain from the sky for you to drink.\" states the result, and it selects drinking as the purpose to mention.\n\nRain has many uses: agriculture, livestock, washing, and the replenishment of wells. The verse names the most basic and the most immediate. A person can live without crops for a while; he cannot live long without water.\n\nSurah al-Waqi'ah presses this point with a question, asking who sends the water down from the clouds, and noting that if Allah willed He could make it bitter, so why are people not grateful.\n\n\"It is not you who hold its reserves.\" closes the verse by connecting it back to the storehouses mentioned a moment earlier.\n\nThe reserves of water are not in human hands. This remains true despite dams, reservoirs, and desalination plants. Every one of those depends on water that arrived from somewhere, and no human arrangement produces a single drop.\n\nThere is a quiet correction in this for a species that has become confident in its ability to manage resources. Managing a supply and creating one are entirely different activities, and the verse points out which of the two people actually do.",
  "23": "\"Surely it is We Who give life and cause death.\" moves the argument from provision to the two events that frame every existence.\n\nThe pairing is deliberate and the order matters. Life comes first, then death, following the sequence a person actually experiences.\n\nBoth are attributed directly to Allah, and this is worth pausing over in each case. Nobody understands how life begins. Biology can describe the processes surrounding it in increasing detail, and the moment at which inert material becomes a living thing remains outside human reach. Nobody can produce it, and nobody can prevent its departure when the time comes.\n\nAttributing death to Allah changes how a believer holds it. Death is not an accident, a system failure, or the final victory of an illness. It arrives at the appointed term this chapter described earlier, which can be neither advanced nor delayed.\n\nThere is also an argument for resurrection built into the statement, and the Quran makes it repeatedly. The One who produced life from nothing is not going to find its restoration difficult.\n\n\"And We are the ˹Eternal˺ Successor.\" closes the verse with a name that carries a striking image.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the one who remains after others are gone, the inheritor. When a person dies, his property passes to those who survive him. The verse extends this to its final conclusion: when everyone has passed, everything returns to the One who remains.\n\nThis is a sobering way to think about ownership. Nobody permanently owns anything. Every possession a person holds is currently between owners, and the sequence of inheritance has a final destination.\n\nSurah Maryam states the same, that Allah will inherit the earth and everyone on it. Surah Ali Imran instructs believers to remember that to Allah belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth.\n\nFor a chapter addressing people absorbed in eating, enjoyment, and long hopes, the reminder is precisely placed.",
  "24": "\"We certainly know those who have gone before you and those who will come after ˹you˺.\" makes a statement about knowledge that spans the whole of human history in both directions.\n\nThe Arabic wording has been understood in more than one way by commentators, and the range is worth noting honestly. Many read it as referring to earlier and later generations of humanity, which is the reading reflected here. Others explained it as those who come forward in the ranks of prayer and those who come later, or those who go forward in doing good and those who lag behind. Some applied it to the ranks of armies.\n\nAll these readings share a single meaning: nobody is unknown, whatever his position in time or in a line.\n\nRead as generations, the verse addresses something a person rarely thinks about. Billions have lived and died, most of them leaving no record at all. Their names are gone, their circumstances forgotten, and no archive holds any trace of them. Billions more are yet to be born.\n\nEvery one of them is known individually. Not as a statistic or a category, but as a person with a life, intentions, and deeds.\n\nThere is comfort in this that is easy to overlook. Human beings fear insignificance, and the fear is reasonable given the numbers involved. A person who lives quietly, harms nobody, does good that is never recorded, and dies without notice has vanished as far as history is concerned.\n\nThe verse says otherwise. Being unrecorded by people is not the same as being unknown, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated that not an atom's weight escapes His knowledge.\n\nThere is also a warning in it for those who assume anonymity protects them. Wrongdoing committed where nobody was watching, in a generation long past or one still to come, is equally present to the One described here.\n\nThe verse that follows draws the practical conclusion: all of them will be gathered.",
  "25": "\"Surely your Lord ˹alone˺ will gather them together ˹for judgment˺.\" follows naturally from the statement that all generations are known.\n\nKnowledge and gathering belong together. A record without an accounting would be pointless, and an accounting without complete knowledge would be unjust. The previous verse established the first, and this one supplies the second.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates collecting and assembling, bringing scattered things into one place. Everyone who has ever lived, from the first human being to the last, in a single gathering.\n\nThat claim raises the obvious objection, which the Quran addresses elsewhere. Bodies have decomposed, been burned, drowned, or scattered across continents over thousands of years. Surah Yasin records a man holding up a decayed bone and asking who will give it life, and the answer given is that the One who produced it the first time will revive it.\n\nThis chapter has already supplied the same argument in a different form, stating a few verses earlier that it is Allah who gives life and causes death.\n\nThe word \"your Lord\" is worth noticing in a verse addressed to the Prophet during a period of intense opposition. The gathering is described as something his Lord will do, which carries assurance rather than only warning. The people mocking him would be present at it.\n\n\"He is truly All-Wise, All-Knowing.\" closes with two names that answer the two questions such a gathering raises.\n\nKnowledge answers whether anything will be missed. Nothing will, since the previous verse established that every generation is known.\n\nWisdom answers whether the outcome will be right. The Arabic indicates placing everything exactly where it belongs, which is what distinguishes a judgement from a mere exercise of power.\n\nTogether they describe a reckoning that is both complete and correct, which is more than any human court has ever managed.",
  "26": "“Indeed, We created man from sounding clay moulded from black mud.”” records the origin of the human creation, and the commentators note the two elements of the description. The verse states the origin: “We created man from sounding clay moulded from black mud,” or “wa laqad khalaqna al-insana min salsalin min hama’in masnoon,” and the commentators explain the two words, the “salsal,” the sounding clay, the clay that has dried and rings when struck, and the “hama’ masnoon,” the black mud, the altered, the moulded, and the description is the stages of the creation, the mud that became the clay, and the commentators note the meaning, for the origin of the human is the low clay, and the low origin is the measure of the divine power, the creation of the honoured from the humble, and the verse is the first of the account of the creation, the introduction of the story of Adam and Iblis that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the origin of the human is the clay, and the clay is the contrast with the fire of the jinn, and the contrast is the ground of the dispute of Iblis, and the verse is the preparation of the story.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the origin, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the origin of the human is the clay, and the remembrance of the origin is the humility, and the one who remembers the clay is the one whose pride the remembrance dissolves, and the believer who knows the origin is the believer whose gratitude the elevation produces, and the low origin is the measure of the mercy, the raising of the clay to the honour of the knowledge and the speech, and the believer who reflects upon the origin is the believer who learns the humility. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the remembrance of the origin: the human is the clay, and the believer who remembers the clay is the believer whose heart the pride cannot capture, and the creation from the humble is the proof of the power of the Lord. The verse is the record of the origin, and the teaching of the humility that the clay demands.",
  "27": "“As for the jinn, We created them earlier from smokeless fire.”” records the origin of the jinn, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the origin: “As for the jinn, We created them earlier from smokeless fire,” or “wal-janna khalaqnahu min qablu min nari as-samoom,” and the commentators explain the two words, the “jann,” the jinn, the hidden ones, and the “nar as-samoom,” the smokeless fire, the scorching wind, and the “earlier” is the priority of the creation, for the jinn were created before the human, and the verse is the contrast, the fire of the jinn against the clay of the human, and the contrast is the ground of the arrogance of Iblis, the comparison that the following verses record, and the commentators note the meaning, for the origin of the jinn is the fire, and the fire is the measure of the claim of Iblis, the claim that the fire is nobler than the clay, and the claim is the error that the story refutes. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the preparation of the dispute, and the dispute is the comparison of the origins, and the comparison is the error, for the nobility of the creature is the obedience, not the substance, and the verse is the record of the origin, the fire that Iblis would boast of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the origin, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own judgment. The believer is taught that the origin is not the measure of the worth, and the one who boasts of the origin is the one whose boast the Lord refutes, and the believer who judges the worth by the obedience is the believer whose judgment the Lord confirms, and the contrast of the fire and the clay is the teaching, for the fire is not nobler than the clay, and the clay was honoured with the knowledge and the fire was cast out for the arrogance, and the believer who reads the contrast is the believer who learns the scale. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the falsehood of the boast: the jinn were the fire and the human was the clay, and the fire was cast out and the clay was honoured, and the believer who judges by the obedience is the believer whose scale is the true. The verse is the record of the origin, and the teaching of the scale that the substance does not set.",
  "28": "“˹Remember, O Prophet˺ when your Lord said to the angels, “I am going to create a human being from sounding clay moulded from black mud.”” records the announcement of the creation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the announcement: “when your Lord said to the angels, ‘I am going to create a human being from sounding clay moulded from black mud,’” or “wa ith qala rabbuka lil-mala’ikati innee khaliqun basharan min salsalin min hama’in masnoon,” and the commentators note the announcement, for the Lord declared to the angels the creation of the human, and the announcement is the beginning of the story, the divine decree that the angels would hear, and the description of the clay is the repetition of the origin, the reminder that the human is the clay, and the announcement is the preparation of the prostration, the command that the following verses record. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the announcement is the first act of the story, and the story is the account of the creation and the prostration and the refusal, and the verse is the introduction of the scene, the declaration of the creation to the angels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the announcement, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the creation of the human was the announced decree, and the announcement to the angels is the proof that the creation is the deliberate act, and the believer who reflects upon the announcement is the believer who learns the dignity of the human, the creature whose creation the Lord announced, and the clay of the origin is the measure of the mercy, the raising of the humble to the honour, and the believer who reads the announcement is the believer whose gratitude the honour produces. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the dignity of the creation: the Lord announced the human, and the believer who reflects upon the announcement is the believer who learns the honour, and the clay is the contrast with the honour, and the contrast is the teaching of the mercy. The verse is the record of the announcement, and the teaching of the dignity that the creation carries.",
  "29": "“So when I have fashioned him and had a spirit of My Own ˹creation˺ breathed into him, fall down in prostration to him.”” records the command of the prostration, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The first is the condition: “So when I have fashioned him and had a spirit of My Own creation breathed into him,” or “fa-itha sawwaytuhu wa nafakhtu feehi min roohee,” and the commentators note the condition, for the Lord declared that when He had fashioned the human and breathed into him of the spirit, the angels should prostrate, and the two acts — the fashioning and the breathing — are the completion of the creation, and the phrase “of My spirit” is the honour, the attribution of the spirit to the Lord, and the commentators note the meaning, for the spirit is the breath of the life, and the attribution is the honour, not the divinity, and the verse is the condition of the prostration. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the breathing of the spirit is the moment of the honour, and the honour is the ground of the prostration, and the verse is the record of the condition, the command that the following verse shows the angels fulfilling. The second part is the command: “fall down in prostration to him,” or “faqa‘oo lahu sajideen,” and the commentators note the command, for the prostration is the act of the honour, the bowing of the angels to the human, and the prostration is the obedience, the acknowledgment of the dignity that the Lord had given, and the verse is the record of the command, the test of the obedience that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prostration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the prostration was the test of the obedience, and the one who prostrated was the one who honoured the command, and the one who refused was the one whose pride the command exposed, and the believer who obeys the command of the Lord is the believer whose prostration the Lord accepts, and the honour of the human is the gift that the believer must not turn into the pride, and the spirit that the Lord breathed is the trust that the believer carries. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the meaning of the obedience: the prostration was the honour of the command, and the believer who obeys is the believer whose honour the obedience secures, and the spirit of the Lord is the gift that the believer must guard. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the prostration tested.",
  "30": "“So the angels prostrated all together—” records the obedience of the angels, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the obedience: “So the angels prostrated all together,” or “fa-sajada al-mala’ikatu kulluhum ajma‘oon,” and the commentators note the words, the “kulluhum ajma‘oon,” the all together, the emphasis of the totality, for the angels prostrated without the exception, and the totality is the measure of the obedience, the host that bowed as one, and the verse is the record of the obedience, the contrast with the refusal that the next verse records, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration of the angels is the proof that the command was the binding, and the obedience of the angels is the model, the submission that the refusal would break. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the totality of the prostration is the emphasis of the obedience, and the emphasis is the contrast, the all against the one, and the contrast is the teaching, the obedience of the many and the refusal of the one, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the obedience that the story sets against the refusal.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the obedience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own submission. The believer is taught that the obedience of the angels is the model, and the totality of the prostration is the measure of the submission, and the believer who obeys the command of the Lord is the believer whose obedience the Lord records, and the prostration of the many is the contrast with the refusal of the one, and the believer who reads the contrast is the believer who chooses the side of the obedience, and the side of the obedience is the side of the safety. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the obedience: the angels prostrated all together, and the believer who obeys is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the totality is the model of the submission that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the obedience, and the teaching of the submission that the command demands.",
  "31": "“but not Iblîs, who refused to prostrate with the others.”” records the refusal of Iblis, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the refusal: “but not Iblis, who refused to prostrate with the others,” or “illa Ibleesa aba an yakoona ma‘a as-sajideen,” and the commentators note the exception, for Iblis was the one who refused, and the word “aba” — he refused — is the description of the will, the deliberate refusal of the command, and the phrase “to be with the prostrating” is the measure, for the refusal was the choice of the separation, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the first act of the arrogance, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal of Iblis is the origin of the rebellion, and the rebellion is the story that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refusal is the will, and the will is the error, and the error is the comparison of the origins, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the exception that the story turns upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refusal, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own will. The believer is taught that the refusal of the command is the choice of the will, and the one who refuses is the one who separates, and the believer who is tempted to the refusal is the believer whose will the temptation tests, and the refusal of Iblis is the warning, the first instance of the pride that the believer must flee, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose will the Lord strengthens, and the separation of Iblis is the end that the believer avoids. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the refusal: Iblis refused and separated, and the believer who refuses the command is the believer whose refusal the Lord records, and the obedience is the side that the believer must choose. The verse is the record of the refusal, and the teaching of the will that the command tests.",
  "32": "“Allah asked, “O Iblîs! What is the matter with you that you did not join others in prostration?”” records the question to Iblis, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse records the question: “O Iblis! What is the matter with you that you did not join others in prostration?” or “qala ya Ibleesu ma laka alla takoona ma‘a as-sajideen,” and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked Iblis the reason of the refusal, and the question is the opening of the examination, the invitation of the confession, and the question is the justice, the asking before the judging, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the answer of Iblis would reveal. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question is the examination, and the examination is the justice, and the answer of Iblis is the confession, and the confession is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the question, the justice that the story displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own examination. The believer is taught that the Lord asks before the judging, and the question is the mercy, the opportunity of the confession, and the believer who is asked is the believer whose answer the Lord hears, and the question of Iblis is the model, the examination that the refusal had earned, and the believer who reflects upon the question is the believer who learns the justice, the asking that precedes the decree. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the Lord: the Lord asked Iblis, and the asking is the mercy, and the believer who is examined is the believer whose answer the Lord weighs, and the question is the opportunity that the refusal had not used. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the justice that the examination displays.",
  "33": "“He replied, “It is not for me to prostrate to a human You created from sounding clay moulded from black mud.”” records the answer of Iblis, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “It is not for me to prostrate to a human You created from sounding clay moulded from black mud,” or “qala lam akun li-asjuda li-basharin khalaqtahu min salsalin min hama’in masnoon,” and the commentators note the answer, for Iblis declared that it was not fitting for him to prostrate to the human of the clay, and the answer is the confession of the arrogance, the comparison of the origins, and the word “bashar” — the human — is the contempt, the reduction of the honour to the substance, and the verse is the record of the answer, the error that the story refutes. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the answer of Iblis is the first argument of the arrogance, the claim that the fire is nobler than the clay, and the claim is the error, for the worth of the creature is the obedience, not the substance, and the verse is the record of the answer, the pride that the following verse judges.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the arrogance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the comparison of the origins is the error, and the one who boasts of the substance is the one whose boast the Lord refutes, and the believer who judges the worth by the obedience is the believer whose scale is the true, and the answer of Iblis is the warning, the first instance of the pride that the believer must flee, and the believer who humbles himself before the command is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the falsehood of the boast: Iblis compared the fire and the clay, and the comparison was the error, and the believer who judges by the obedience is the believer whose judgment the Lord confirms, and the humility of the believer is the answer to the arrogance of Iblis. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the humility that the boast opposes.",
  "34": "“Allah commanded, “Then get out of Paradise, for you are truly cursed.”” records the sentence upon Iblis, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “Then get out of Paradise, for you are truly cursed,” or “qala fakhruj minha fa-innaka rajeem,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded Iblis to depart from the Paradise, and the word “rajeem” — the cursed, the stoned, the cast out — is the sentence, the expulsion and the curse, and the command is the judgment, the answer to the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the sentence, the first of the judgments that the story records. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the expulsion is the judgment, and the judgment is the justice, and the curse is the recompense of the pride, and the verse is the record of the sentence, the expulsion that the arrogance earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sentence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the arrogance is the sin that the expulsion answers, and the one who boasts of the origin is the one whom the Lord casts out, and the believer who humbles himself is the believer whom the Lord receives, and the sentence upon Iblis is the warning, the end of the pride that the believer must flee, and the curse is the recompense that the believer avoids by the humility. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the pride: Iblis was cast out and cursed, and the believer who flees the pride is the believer whose station the Lord preserves, and the sentence is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the sentence, and the teaching of the fear that the pride should produce.",
  "35": "“And surely upon you is condemnation until the Day of Judgment.”” continues the sentence, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the continuation: “And surely upon you is condemnation until the Day of Judgment,” or “wa inna ‘alayka al-la‘nata ila yawmi ad-deen,” and the commentators note the “la‘nah,” the condemnation, the curse, the expulsion from the mercy, and the duration, the “yawm ad-deen,” the Day of Judgment, and the verse is the completion of the sentence, the declaration that the curse is upon Iblis until the day of the reckoning, and the commentators note the significance, for the duration of the curse is the measure of the fall, the pride that earned the eternal condemnation, and the verse is the record of the sentence, the curse that the arrogance had purchased. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the “la‘nah” is the distance from the mercy, and the distance is the recompense, and the day of the judgment is the term, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the curse that the story traces to the end.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the curse, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the curse is the distance from the mercy, and the one who earns the curse is the one whose pride has severed the mercy, and the believer who fears the curse is the believer who guards the humility, and the duration of the curse is the warning, the permanence of the fall that the believer must avoid, and the condemnation of Iblis is the measure of the end that the arrogance invites. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the permanence of the recompense: the curse is upon Iblis until the Day, and the believer who flees the pride is the believer whose distance the Lord spares, and the curse is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the fear that the curse should produce.",
  "36": "“Satan appealed, “My Lord! Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.”” records the appeal of Iblis, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The verse records the appeal: “Satan appealed, ‘My Lord! Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection,’” or “qala rabbi fa-anzirnee ila yawmi yub‘athoon,” and the commentators note the appeal, for Iblis asked the Lord to delay his end until the day of the resurrection, and the appeal is the request of the respite, the seeking of the time, and the commentators note the significance, for the request is the beginning of the enmity, the declaration that Iblis would use the time for the misguidance of the children of Adam, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the step that the answer of the Lord would qualify. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appeal is the request of the time, and the time is the means of the temptation, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the beginning of the war that the following verses describe.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the appeal, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own awareness. The believer is taught that the enemy of the believer is the one who requested the time, and the time is the span of the temptation, and the believer who knows the request is the believer who knows the war, and the awareness of the enemy is the first of the weapons, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the enemy is the believer whose guard the Lord strengthens, and the appeal of Iblis is the warning, the declaration of the enmity that the believer must face. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the enemy: Iblis asked the time for the temptation, and the believer who knows the request is the believer who prepares the guard, and the time of the world is the span of the struggle. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the enmity that the request declares.",
  "37": "“Allah said, “You will be delayed”” records the answer of the Lord, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the answer: “You will be delayed,” or “qala fa-innaka mina al-munzareen,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord granted the delay, and the granting is the permission, the allowance of the time, and the verse is the record of the answer, the first half of the reply that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the delay is the test, the allowance of the enemy that the trial of the human requires, and the verse is the record of the granting, the time that the following verse bounds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the delay is the permission, and the permission is the test, and the test is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the answer, the granting that the Lord qualified.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the delay, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own understanding. The believer is taught that the delay of the enemy is the test, and the allowance of the time is the wisdom, and the believer who knows the delay is the believer who knows the trial, and the time of the enemy is the span of the struggle, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the test is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the delay of Iblis is the measure of the test that the believer faces. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the delay: the enemy was granted the time, and the time is the test, and the believer who knows the test is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the delay is the span of the struggle that the believer must endure. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the test that the delay allows.",
  "38": "“until the appointed Day.”” completes the answer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the answer: “until the appointed Day,” or “ila yawmi al-waqti al-ma‘loom,” and the commentators note the “waqt ma‘loom,” the appointed time, the known term, and the verse is the qualification of the delay, the declaration that the time of the enemy is bounded, and the commentators note the significance, for the term of the enemy is known to the Lord and hidden from the enemy, and the bound is the mercy, the assurance that the time of the temptation has its end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the term that the delay is bounded by. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appointed Day is the term, and the term is the bound, and the bound is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the completion, the qualification that the delay received.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the term, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the time of the enemy is bounded, and the temptation has its end, and the believer who knows the bound is the believer whose patience is the hopeful, and the term of the enemy is the comfort, the assurance that the struggle is the temporary, and the believer who endures the trial is the believer whose end the Lord has appointed, and the appointed Day is the promise that the time of the enemy will cease. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the bound of the trial: the enemy was given the time and the term, and the believer who knows the term is the believer whose patience the hope sustains, and the appointed Day is the promise that the struggle has its end. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the term that the delay is bounded by.",
  "39": "“Satan responded, “My Lord! For allowing me to stray I will surely tempt them on earth and mislead them all together,”” records the vow of Iblis, and the commentators note the two parts of the vow. The verse records the vow: “My Lord! For allowing me to stray I will surely tempt them on earth and mislead them all together,” or “qala rabbi bima aghwaytanee la-uzayyinanna lahum fi al-ardi wa la-ughwiyannahum ajma‘een,” and the commentators note the vow, for Iblis blamed the Lord for the straying, and the blame is the confession of the corruption, the shifting of the fault, and the vow is the declaration of the war, the promise to tempt and to mislead, and the word “ajma‘een” — all together — is the scope of the vow, the claim of the total, and the verse is the record of the vow, the beginning of the campaign that the following verse qualifies. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the vow is the enmity, and the blame is the corruption, and the scope of the vow is the measure of the malice, and the verse is the record of the vow, the war that the enemy declared.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the vow, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own awareness. The believer is taught that the enemy of the believer is the one who vowed the total, and the vow is the declaration of the war, and the believer who knows the vow is the believer who knows the enemy, and the awareness of the enemy is the first of the weapons, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the war is the believer whose guard the Lord strengthens, and the vow of Iblis is the warning, the declaration of the campaign that the believer must face. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scope of the enmity: Iblis vowed the total, and the believer who knows the vow is the believer who prepares the guard, and the war is the reality that the believer must acknowledge. The verse is the record of the vow, and the teaching of the enmity that the believer must face.",
  "40": "“except Your chosen servants among them.”” completes the vow with the exception, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the vow: “except Your chosen servants among them,” or “illa ‘ibadaka minhum al-mukhlaseen,” and the commentators note the exception, for Iblis confessed that the chosen servants, the “mukhlaseen,” the purified, the sincere, are the ones whom his vow cannot reach, and the exception is the confession, the acknowledgment of the protection of the sincere, and the verse is the record of the exception, the limit that the enemy himself declared, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the key, the declaration that the sincerity is the shield, and the shield is the thing that the believer must seek, and the verse is the record of the completion, the limit that the vow of the enemy carries. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the “mukhlaseen” are the sincere, and the sincerity is the protection, and the exception is the confession of the enemy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the shield that the enemy acknowledged.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sincerity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own shield. The believer is taught that the sincerity is the shield, and the one whom the Lord purifies is the one whom the enemy cannot reach, and the believer who seeks the sincerity is the believer whose shield the Lord grants, and the exception of Iblis is the confession, the declaration that the sincere are the safe, and the believer who lives the sincerity is the believer whose safety the enemy himself acknowledged. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the key of the safety: the sincere are the safe, and the believer who purifies the heart is the believer whom the enemy cannot tempt, and the exception is the promise that the sincerity is the protection. The verse is the record of the exception, and the teaching of the sincerity that the enemy cannot reach.",
  "41": "“Allah said, “This is the Way, binding on Me:”” records the declaration of the Lord, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the declaration: “This is the Way, binding on Me,” or “qala hatha siratun ‘alayya mustaqeem,” and the commentators note the words, the “sirat mustaqeem,” the straight way, and the “alayya,” the binding upon Me, and the declaration is the divine commitment, the assurance that the protection of the sincere is the way that the Lord has bound Himself to, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the first half of the statement that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the promise, and the promise is the comfort of the sincere, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the commitment that the Lord made. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the straight way is the way of the protection, and the binding is the commitment, and the commitment is the promise, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the promise that the following verse applies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the promise, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the protection of the sincere is the promise of the Lord, and the promise is the commitment, and the believer who walks the straight way is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the promise is the comfort of the one whose sincerity the enemy threatens, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose trust the Lord honours, and the way of the Lord is the safety that the believer seeks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the protection: the Lord declared the way, and the believer who walks the way is the believer whom the Lord guards, and the promise is the comfort that the believer carries. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the promise that the sincere trust.",
  "42": "“you will certainly have no authority over My servants, except the deviant who follow you,”” completes the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the declaration: “you will certainly have no authority over My servants, except the deviant who follow you,” or “inna ‘ibadee laysa laka ‘alayhim sultanun illa mani ittaba‘aka mina al-ghaween,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the negation of the authority, and the exception of the deviant, and the verse is the declaration of the limit, the teaching that the enemy has no authority over the servants of the Lord, and the authority is only over the deviant who follow, and the commentators note the significance, for the authority of the enemy is not the compulsion but the following, and the following is the choice of the deviant, and the verse is the record of the completion, the limit that the following sets. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the enemy has no authority over the sincere, and the deviant are the ones who gave the authority by the following, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the choice is the servant’s.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the authority, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own responsibility. The believer is taught that the enemy has no authority over the servant, and the following is the choice, and the believer who follows the truth is the believer whom the enemy cannot touch, and the believer who follows the deviance is the believer who gave the authority, and the responsibility is the servant’s, and the believer who knows the limit is the believer whose choice the Lord guides. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the freedom of the will: the enemy has no authority, and the following is the choice, and the believer who chooses the truth is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the deviant are the ones who chose the enemy. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the choice that the authority respects.",
  "43": "“and surely Hell is their destined place, all together.”” records the destination of the deviant, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the destination: “and surely Hell is their destined place, all together,” or “wa inna jahannama la-maw‘iduhum ajma‘een,” and the commentators note the “maw‘id,” the appointment, the destined place, and the “ajma‘een,” the all together, and the verse is the declaration of the end, the Fire that the deviant and their leader share, and the commentators note the significance, for the gathering of the deviant and the enemy in the Fire is the justice, the recompense of the following, and the verse is the record of the destination, the end that the deviance earns. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Fire is the appointment, and the appointment is the justice, and the gathering is the recompense, and the verse is the record of the destination, the end that the following leads to.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destination, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the end of the deviance is the Fire, and the one who follows the enemy is the one whose appointment is the Fire, and the believer who fears the end is the believer who avoids the following, and the gathering of the deviant and the enemy is the warning, the justice that the believer must heed, and the believer who follows the truth is the believer whose appointment the Lord separates from the Fire. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the deviance: the Fire is the appointment, and the believer who follows the truth is the believer whose appointment the Lord makes the Paradise, and the gathering of the deviant is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the destination, and the teaching of the fear that the end should produce.",
  "44": "“It has seven gates, to each a group of them is designated.”” describes the Fire, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse describes the Fire: “It has seven gates, to each a group of them is designated,” or “laha sab‘atu abwabin li-kulli babin minhum juz’un maqsoom,” and the commentators note the “sab‘at abwab,” the seven gates, and the “juz’ maqsoom,” the designated portion, and the verse is the description, the declaration that the Fire has the seven gates and the groups are distributed, and the commentators note the significance, for the seven gates are the levels, and the distribution is the justice, the grouping of the deviant by their deeds, and the verse is the record of the description, the detail that the threat of the Fire carries. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the seven gates are the seven levels, and the distribution is the justice, and the verse is the record of the description, the detail that the believer must heed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the detail, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the Fire is the reality, and the detail of the gates is the vividness, and the believer who reflects upon the description is the believer whose fear the detail sharpens, and the distribution of the groups is the justice, the declaration that the end is measured, and the believer who reads the description is the believer who prepares the avoidance, and the seven gates are the warning that the believer must not forget. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the Fire: the seven gates and the groups, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose fear the detail deepens, and the description is the mercy, the warning that the vividness makes the effective. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the fear that the detail produces.",
  "45": "“Indeed, the righteous will be amid Gardens and springs.”” records the destination of the righteous, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the destination: “Indeed, the righteous will be amid Gardens and springs,” or “inna al-muttaqeena fee jannatin wa ‘uyoon,” and the commentators note the “muttaqeen,” the righteous, the God-fearing, and the “jannat wa ‘uyoon,” the gardens and the springs, and the verse is the declaration of the end, the Paradise that the righteous inherit, and the commentators note the contrast, for the verse follows the description of the Fire with the description of the Paradise, and the contrast is the teaching, the two ends that the two ways lead to, and the verse is the record of the destination, the end that the taqwa earns. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the righteous are the opposite of the deviant, and the gardens and the springs are the opposite of the Fire, and the verse is the record of the contrast, the two ends that the believer must choose between.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destination, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the end of the taqwa is the Paradise, and the one who fears the Lord is the one whom the Lord receives in the gardens and the springs, and the believer who hopes for the Paradise is the believer who cultivates the taqwa, and the contrast of the two ends is the warning and the promise, and the believer who reads the two verses is the believer who chooses the taqwa, and the gardens and the springs are the reward that the believer seeks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the taqwa: the righteous are in the gardens, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose end the Lord makes the Paradise, and the contrast is the teaching that the two ways lead to the two ends. The verse is the record of the destination, and the teaching of the hope that the taqwa carries.",
  "46": "“˹It will be said to them,˺ “Enter in peace and security.”” records the greeting of the righteous, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the greeting: “Enter in peace and security,” or “udkhulooha bi-salamin amineen,” and the commentators note the two words, the “salam,” the peace, and the “amin,” the security, and the verse is the greeting, the reception of the righteous with the peace and the safety, and the commentators note the significance, for the greeting is the answer to the fear, the declaration that the Paradise is the place of the peace, and the verse is the record of the greeting, the reception that the following verse describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the peace and the security are the two gifts of the entry, and the gifts are the opposite of the fear of the world, and the verse is the record of the greeting, the reception that the taqwa earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the greeting, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the end of the righteous is the peace, and the entry into the Paradise is the reception with the security, and the believer who fears the Lord in the world is the believer whose end is the peace, and the greeting is the promise, the declaration that the fear of the world is followed by the safety of the next, and the believer who hopes for the greeting is the believer who lives the taqwa. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the taqwa: the greeting of the peace, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose entry the Lord makes the peaceful, and the security is the reward of the fear. The verse is the record of the greeting, and the teaching of the peace that the taqwa earns.",
  "47": "“We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts. In a friendly manner, they will be on thrones, facing one another.”” records the purification of the hearts, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the removal: “We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts,” or “wa naza‘na ma fee sudoorihim min ghillin,” and the commentators note the “ghill,” the bitterness, the rancour, the hidden grudge, and the removal, for the Lord will remove the rancour from the hearts of the righteous, and the removal is the purification, the cleansing of the hearts from the grudges of the world, and the verse is the description of the Paradise, the peace that the removal of the rancour secures. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rancour is the poison of the world, and the removal is the healing, and the verse is the record of the purification, the cleansing that the Paradise grants. The second part is the fellowship: “In a friendly manner, they will be on thrones, facing one another,” or “ikhwanan ‘ala sururin mutaqabileen,” and the commentators note the “ikhwan,” the brothers, and the “surur,” the thrones, and the “mutaqabileen,” the facing one another, and the verse is the description, the fellowship of the righteous, the brotherhood that the removal of the rancour makes possible, and the commentators note the beauty, for the facing of one another is the intimacy, the company that the Paradise grants.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the purification, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own heart. The believer is taught that the rancour is the poison, and the removal is the healing, and the believer who cleans the heart in the world is the believer whose heart the Lord purifies in the next, and the brotherhood of the righteous is the reward, the company that the purification secures, and the believer who forgives in the world is the believer whose heart the Lord makes the pure, and the facing of one another is the intimacy that the believer hopes. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the clean heart: the removal of the rancour is the purification, and the believer who forgives is the believer whose heart the Lord cleans, and the brotherhood of the thrones is the reward of the clean heart. The verse is the record of the purification, and the teaching of the heart that the Paradise cleans.",
  "48": "“No fatigue will touch them there, nor will they ever be asked to leave.”” completes the description of the Paradise, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the description: “No fatigue will touch them there, nor will they ever be asked to leave,” or “la yamassuhum feeha nasabun wa ma hum minha bi-mukhrijeen,” and the commentators note the two negations, the “nasab,” the fatigue, and the “ikhraj,” the expulsion, and the verse is the declaration, the assurance that the Paradise is the place of the rest and the permanence, and the commentators note the significance, for the two negations are the answer to the two fears, the fear of the toil and the fear of the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the permanence that the following verses contrast with the story of the guests. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Paradise is the place of the rest, and the rest is the reward, and the permanence is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the peace that the taqwa earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the permanence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the Paradise is the rest and the permanence, and the one who fears the Lord is the one whom the Lord grants the rest, and the believer who hopes for the Paradise is the believer who endures the toil of the world, and the two negations are the promise, the assurance that the toil ends and the permanence begins, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the peace of the end: no fatigue and no expulsion, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose end the Lord makes the permanent, and the rest is the reward of the taqwa. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the permanence that the Paradise grants.",
  "49": "\"Inform My servants ˹O Prophet˺ that I am truly the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,\" is an instruction to deliver a message, and the message is about Allah Himself.\n\nThe two names are among the most frequently paired in the Quran. The first indicates extensive, repeated forgiveness, covering what has been done. The second indicates continuous mercy that reaches those who receive it.\n\nNotice who is being addressed. They are called My servants, which is a term of relationship rather than accusation. The verse does not open by naming their failures.\n\nThe instruction to inform is worth pausing over. This is not presented as information people naturally possess. It has to be told to them, which suggests that human beings do not arrive at an accurate view of Allah's mercy on their own.\n\nThat observation holds up. People tend to construct a picture of God from their own experience of authority, and human authority is generally conditional, easily offended, and slow to forgive. A person who has been treated that way by parents, employers, or communities projects the same pattern upward.\n\nThe Prophet corrected this repeatedly, and the hadith literature is full of it. He described Allah as more pleased with a servant's repentance than a man who loses his camel with all his provisions in a desert and then finds it again, in a report recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nThere is also something significant about the placement of this verse. It comes immediately after the descriptions of Paradise, at the point where a reader might feel that such a reward belongs to people better than himself.\n\nDespair is what the verse is aimed at, and the chapter will state this explicitly a few verses later when Abraham asks who despairs of the mercy of his Lord except those who have gone astray.\n\nThe balance to this announcement follows immediately in the next verse.",
  "50": "\"And that My torment is indeed the most painful.\" completes the message begun in the previous verse, and the pairing is the whole point.\n\nThe Prophet was instructed to inform people of two things, and neither is delivered without the other. Forgiveness and mercy were announced first; the severity of the punishment is announced second, in the same breath and as part of the same instruction.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the painful punishment, and the construction gives it emphasis.\n\nThis pairing runs through the entire Quran, and it exists because either half taken alone produces a distortion.\n\nA picture of Allah built only from mercy produces carelessness. If forgiveness is automatic and unconditional regardless of conduct, then warnings mean nothing, injustice has no consequence, and the whole moral structure collapses. People who hold this view tend to postpone repentance indefinitely, since nothing appears to be at stake.\n\nA picture built only from severity produces despair. A person who believes he is dealing with an authority looking for reasons to punish will eventually stop trying, and the Quran treats that despair as a serious spiritual failure rather than as humility.\n\nThe Prophet expressed the balance in a hadith recorded by Muslim, saying that if a believer knew the extent of Allah's punishment, nobody would hope for His Paradise, and if a disbeliever knew the extent of His mercy, nobody would despair of it.\n\nScholars have long described the believer's state as flying between two wings: hope and fear. Losing either one makes flight impossible.\n\nThere is something instructive in the order used here. Mercy came first. That order appears consistently in the Quran, and the Prophet reported that Allah wrote a decree about Himself which is with Him above the Throne, stating that His mercy prevails over His wrath, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari.",
  "51": "“And inform them ˹O Prophet˺ about Abraham’s guests”” opens the account of the guests, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the command: “And inform them O Prophet about Abraham’s guests,” or “wa nabbi’hum ‘an dayfi Ibraheem,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to inform the people of the story of the guests of Abraham, and the command is the introduction, the opening of the account that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the story of the guests is the story of the mercy and the punishment, the glad tidings of the son and the warning of the destruction, and the verse is the record of the command, the invitation to the reflection upon the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the guests are the angels, and the story is the account of their visit to Abraham and their mission to the people of Lot, and the verse is the record of the command, the introduction of the account.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the informing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the story of the guests is the lesson, and the one who is informed is the one who reflects, and the believer who reads the account is the believer who learns the mercy and the warning, and the command to the Prophet is the invitation to the believer, the call to the reflection upon the guests and their mission, and the believer who reflects upon the account is the believer whose heart the account softens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the account: the story of the guests is the lesson, and the believer who reads it is the believer who learns, and the informing is the mercy that the reflection receives. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the reflection that the account invites.",
  "52": "“who entered upon him and greeted ˹him with˺, “Peace!” He ˹later˺ said, “Surely we are afraid of you.”” records the entry and the fear, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the entry: “who entered upon him and greeted him with, ‘Peace!’” or “ith dakhaloo ‘alayhi fa-qaloo salaman,” and the commentators note the greeting, for the angels entered upon Abraham and offered the peace, and the greeting is the first exchange, the courtesy of the guests, and the verse records the greeting as the opening of the visit. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the guests were the angels in the form of the men, and the greeting was the peace, and the verse is the record of the entry, the courtesy that the guests offered. The second part is the fear: “He later said, ‘Surely we are afraid of you,’” or “qala inna minkum wajiloon,” and the commentators note the fear, for Abraham was afraid of the guests, and the fear is the alarm of the host, for the guests did not eat, and the refusal of the food was the sign that alarmed the host, and the verse is the record of the fear, the alarm that the following verses calm, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear of Abraham is the human reaction, and the reaction is the teaching, the alarm that the revelation of the mission would dissolve.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own alarm. The believer is taught that the alarm of the heart is the human, and the one who is alarmed by the unknown is the one whom the Lord calms, and the believer who fears is the believer whose fear the reassurance dissolves, and the fear of Abraham is the model, the alarm that the declaration of the angels would remove, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the fear is the believer whose fear the Lord turns to the peace. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the human fear and the divine calm: Abraham feared and was reassured, and the believer who fears the unknown is the believer whom the Lord calms, and the fear of the host is the prelude of the good news. The verse is the record of the entry and the fear, and the teaching of the alarm that the reassurance dissolves.",
  "53": "“They reassured ˹him˺, “Do not be afraid! Surely we give you good news of a knowledgeable son.”” records the reassurance and the news, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the reassurance: “Do not be afraid!” or “qaloo la tawjal,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the angels calmed the fear of Abraham, and the reassurance is the first word of the mission, the removal of the alarm, and the verse records the reassurance as the calm that the fear received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reassurance is the comfort, and the comfort is the prelude of the news, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the calm that the angels offered. The second part is the news: “Surely we give you good news of a knowledgeable son,” or “inna nubashshiruka bi-ghulamin ‘aleem,” and the commentators note the news, for the angels announced the birth of a son, and the son is described as the “ghulam ‘aleem,” the knowledgeable boy, and the description is the prophecy of the station, the son who would be the prophet, and the news is the glad tiding, the announcement of the Isaac, and the verse is the record of the news, the good tidings that the following verses confirm.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the news, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the good news of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who receives the news is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who is given the promise is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the news of the son is the proof that the mercy of the Lord is not bound by the age, and the believer who despairs of the mercy has forgotten the news that the angels brought. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scope of the mercy: the news came despite the age, and the believer who trusts the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the son is the promise that the patience received. The verse is the record of the reassurance and the news, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy grants.",
  "54": "“He wondered, “Do you give me good news despite my old age? What unlikely news!”” records the wonder of Abraham, and the commentators note the two parts of the wonder. The first is the question: “Do you give me good news despite my old age?” or “qala a-bashshartumoonee ‘ala an massaniya al-kibaru,” and the commentators note the question, for Abraham wondered at the news of the son in the old age, and the question is the astonishment, the human reaction of the one whose age had closed the door of the expectation, and the verse records the question as the measure of the mercy, the news that the age could not bind. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the wonder, and the wonder is the human, and the verse is the record of the question, the astonishment that the following verse corrects. The second part is the exclamation: “What unlikely news!” or “fa-bi-ma tubashshiroon,” and the commentators note the exclamation, for Abraham declared the unlikeliness of the news, and the exclamation is the measure of the astonishment, the declaration that the news exceeded the ordinary, and the verse is the record of the wonder, the astonishment that the confirmation of the angels would answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wonder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own astonishment. The believer is taught that the promise of the Lord is the news that the reason finds astonishing, and the wonder of Abraham is the human, and the correction of the wonder is the teaching, the declaration that the decree of the Lord is the thing that the reason cannot bind, and the believer who wonders at the promise must hear the answer, the reminder that the mercy of the Lord is the astonishing thing that the reason must yield to. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the limit of the reason: Abraham reasoned from the age and the Lord promised the son, and the believer who hears the promise must not confine it to the measure of the reason, and the wonder is the prelude of the faith. The verse is the record of the wonder, and the teaching of the astonishment that the promise exceeds.",
  "55": "“They responded, “We give you good news in all truth, so do not be one of those who despair.”” records the confirmation of the angels, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the confirmation: “We give you good news in all truth,” or “qaloo bashsharnaka bil-haqqi,” and the commentators note the confirmation, for the angels affirmed the truth of the news, and the affirmation is the assurance, the declaration that the news was not the wonder but the truth, and the verse records the confirmation as the answer to the astonishment, the assurance that the promise was the real. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the truth of the news is the affirmation, and the affirmation is the comfort, and the verse is the record of the confirmation, the assurance that the following verses complete. The second part is the warning: “so do not be one of those who despair,” or “fa-la takun mina al-qaniteen,” and the commentators note the warning, for the angels warned Abraham against the despair, and the warning is the teaching, the declaration that the despair of the mercy is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confirmation and the warning that the following verse answers with the confession of the hope.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own despair. The believer is taught that the despair of the mercy is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the one who despairs is the one whose hope the Lord corrects, and the believer who holds the hope is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the warning of the angels is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer must never despair of, and the believer who is tempted to the despair is the believer whom the warning recalls. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prohibition of the despair: the angels warned Abraham, and the believer who despairs is the believer whom the warning corrects, and the hope is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the hope that the despair opposes.",
  "56": "“He exclaimed, “Who would despair of the mercy of their Lord except the misguided?”” records the confession of Abraham, and the commentators note the two parts of the exclamation. The verse records the exclamation: “Who would despair of the mercy of their Lord except the misguided?” or “qala wa man yaqnatu min rahmati rabbihi illa ad-dalloon,” and the commentators note the confession, for Abraham declared that the despair of the mercy is the mark of the misguided, and the declaration is the faith, the certainty that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer never despairs of, and the verse is the record of the confession, the answer to the warning of the angels, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession of Abraham is the statement of the hope, and the hope is the thing that the following verse completes with the question of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the despair is the misguidance, and the hope is the guidance, and the verse is the record of the confession, the faith that the mercy is the certain.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the despair of the mercy is the misguidance, and the hope in the mercy is the guidance, and the believer who holds the hope is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the confession of Abraham is the model, the declaration that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer never despairs of, and the believer who is tempted to the despair is the believer whom the confession recalls, and the mercy of the Lord is the hope that the believer carries. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the mercy: Abraham declared the hope, and the believer who holds the hope is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the despair is the misguidance that the believer must flee. The verse is the record of the confession, and the teaching of the hope that the despair opposes.",
  "57": "“He ˹then˺ added, “What is your mission, O messenger-angels?”” records the question of Abraham, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “What is your mission, O messenger-angels?” or “qala fa-ma khatbukum ayyuha al-mursaloon,” and the commentators note the question, for Abraham asked the angels of their mission, and the question is the turn of the scene, the move from the good news to the warning, and the verse records the question as the opening of the account of the destruction, the inquiry that the following verses answer, and the commentators note the significance, for the question of Abraham is the prelude of the intercession, the concern of the friend of God for the people of Lot, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the answer of the angels would take. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the inquiry, and the inquiry is the concern, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the account of the destruction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the concern, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own care. The believer is taught that the concern for the people is the mark of the believer, and the one who asks of the fate of the others is the one whose heart the mercy has not left, and the believer who cares for the community is the believer whose care the Lord values, and the question of Abraham is the model, the inquiry that the compassion produced, and the believer who is informed of the warning is the believer whose concern the Lord records. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the concern of the believer: Abraham asked of the mission, and the believer who asks of the fate is the believer whose heart the mercy has kept, and the question is the prelude of the intercession that the following verses record. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the concern that the inquiry reveals.",
  "58": "“They replied, “We have actually been sent to a wicked people.”” records the answer of the angels, and the commentators note the two elements of the reply. The verse records the reply: “We have actually been sent to a wicked people,” or “qaloo inna ursilna ila qawmin mujrimeen,” and the commentators note the reply, for the angels declared their mission to the “mujrimeen,” the wicked, the criminals, and the declaration is the announcement, the statement that the angels were sent to the people of Lot, and the verse is the record of the reply, the opening of the account of the destruction, and the commentators note the significance, for the word “mujrimeen” is the verdict, the description of the people of Lot as the criminals, and the verdict is the ground of the punishment that the following verses record. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the punishment, and the punishment is the recompense of the crime, and the verse is the record of the reply, the announcement of the destruction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the wicked are the ones whom the punishment is sent to, and the one who persists in the sin is the one whom the mission of the punishment finds, and the believer who fears the punishment is the believer who avoids the sin, and the word “mujrimeen” is the warning, the description of the end that the sin earns, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose fear the description sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the sin: the angels were sent to the wicked, and the believer who avoids the sin is the believer whom the punishment spares, and the mission is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the fear that the mission produces.",
  "59": "“As for the family of Lot, we will certainly deliver them all,”” records the exception of the salvation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the exception: “As for the family of Lot, we will certainly deliver them all,” or “illa ala Lootin inna la-munajjoohum ajma‘een,” and the commentators note the exception, for the family of Lot is the exception to the destruction, and the salvation is the deliverance, the “najah,” the rescue, and the word “ajma‘een” — all — is the scope, the deliverance of the whole family, and the verse is the record of the exception, the salvation that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the deliverance of the family is the mercy, and the mercy is the answer to the faith, and the verse is the record of the exception, the salvation that the faith earned. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the family of Lot is the saved, and the salvation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the exception, the deliverance that the following verse limits.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the salvation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the salvation of the family is the mercy, and the one who is of the family of the faith is the one whom the Lord delivers, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the exception of Lot is the promise, the assurance that the faithful are the delivered, and the believer who reads the exception is the believer whose hope the salvation sustains. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the deliverance: the family of Lot was saved, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whom the Lord delivers, and the salvation is the reward of the faith. The verse is the record of the exception, and the teaching of the hope that the salvation grants.",
  "60": "“except his wife. We have determined that she will be one of the doomed.”” completes the qualification, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the qualification: “except his wife. We have determined that she will be one of the doomed,” or “illa imra’atahu qaddarna innaha la-mina al-ghabireen,” and the commentators note the exception, for the wife of Lot is the exception to the salvation, and the decree is the determination, the “qaddarna,” the ordaining, and the word “ghabireen” — the doomed, the remaining behind — is the description, and the verse is the record of the exception, the declaration that the wife is not of the saved, and the commentators note the significance, for the wife of the prophet is the one whose heart was with the doomed, and the verse is the teaching, for the salvation is the faith, not the kinship, and the wife who did not believe is the wife who was not saved. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the wife of Lot was the one who conspired with her people, and the exception is the justice, and the verse is the record of the exception, the teaching that the kinship does not save.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kinship, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the salvation is the faith, and the kinship of the prophet does not save the one who does not believe, and the wife of Lot is the warning, the proof that the bond of the blood is not the bond of the salvation, and the believer who relies upon the kinship is the believer whose reliance the verse corrects, and the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whom the Lord saves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the condition of the salvation: the wife was not saved, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whom the Lord delivers, and the kinship is the thing that the salvation does not weigh. The verse is the record of the exception, and the teaching of the faith that the salvation requires.",
  "61": "“So when the messengers came to the family of Lot,” records the arrival of the angels at the house of Lot, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the arrival: “So when the messengers came to the family of Lot,” or “fa-lamma ja’a ala Lootin al-mursaloon,” and the commentators note the arrival, for the angels, having departed from Abraham, came to the family of Lot, and the arrival is the opening of the scene, the entry of the messengers into the house of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the step that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the arrival is the fulfillment of the mission, and the mission is the deliverance and the destruction, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the opening of the account of the night of the salvation. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the messengers came to Lot in the form of the handsome young men, and the arrival is the test of the people, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the prelude of the events that the following verses describe.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the arrival, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own providence. The believer is taught that the arrival of the messengers is the arrival of the mercy, and the one whom the messengers visit is the one whom the Lord delivers, and the believer who is in the trial is the believer whose deliverance the Lord has appointed, and the arrival of the angels is the promise, the assurance that the salvation comes at the hour, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the trial is the believer whose hour the Lord sets. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the arrival: the messengers came, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose messengers the Lord sends, and the arrival is the prelude of the deliverance that the following verses record. The verse is the record of the arrival, and the teaching of the providence that the mission carries.",
  "62": "“he said, “You are surely an unfamiliar people!”” records the words of Lot, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the words: “You are surely an unfamiliar people!” or “qala innakum qawmun munkaroon,” and the commentators note the words, for Lot said to the angels that they were an unfamiliar people, and the words are the fear, the anxiety of the host who knew the danger that the guests would face, and the verse is the record of the words, the alarm that the following verses calm, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear of Lot is the same fear that the arrival of the guests had caused, and the fear is the measure of the wickedness of the town, and the verse is the record of the words, the anxiety of the host. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the unfamiliarity is the fear, and the fear is the knowledge of the town, and the verse is the record of the words, the alarm that the declaration of the angels would dissolve.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own anxiety. The believer is taught that the fear of the host for the guest is the mark of the honour, and the one who fears for the safety of the guest is the one whose hospitality the Lord values, and the believer who is anxious for the other is the believer whose anxiety the Lord calms, and the fear of Lot is the model, the anxiety that the declaration of the mission would turn to the relief, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the fear is the believer whose fear the Lord resolves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the host: Lot feared for the guests, and the believer who fears for the other is the believer whose concern the Lord values, and the fear of Lot is the prelude of the reassurance that the following verse records. The verse is the record of the words, and the teaching of the anxiety that the honour produces.",
  "63": "“They responded, “We have come to you with that ˹torment˺ which they have doubted.”” records the first part of the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reply: “We have come to you with that torment which they have doubted,” or “qaloo bal ji’naka bima kanoo feehi yamtaroon,” and the commentators note the reply, for the angels declared that they had come with the torment that the people had doubted, and the declaration is the revelation of the mission, the announcement that the punishment that the people had mocked was now the mission of the guests, and the verse is the record of the reply, the first part of the reassurance that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the doubt of the people is the thing that the arrival would answer, and the answer is the torment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the announcement of the punishment. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the doubt is the mockery, and the punishment is the answer, and the verse is the record of the reply, the declaration of the mission.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the doubt, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the doubt of the punishment is the thing that the punishment answers, and the one who mocks the warning is the one whom the warning overtakes, and the believer who is certain of the punishment is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of the angels is the warning, the declaration that the doubted punishment is the coming reality, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose certainty the declaration strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the warning: the doubted punishment came, and the believer who trusts the warning is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the doubt of the people is the thing that the arrival answered. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the certainty that the warning demands.",
  "64": "“We come to you with the truth, and we are certainly truthful.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the reply: “We come to you with the truth, and we are certainly truthful,” or “wa ataynaka bil-haqqi wa inna la-sadiqoon,” and the commentators note the two declarations, the truth and the truthfulness, and the verse is the completion, the assurance that the mission is the truth and the messengers are the truthful, and the commentators note the significance, for the truth is the declaration of the decree, and the truthfulness is the assurance of the messengers, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reassurance that the fear of Lot received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the truth is the decree, and the truthfulness is the honesty, and the verse is the record of the completion, the assurance that the following verses apply.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the truth, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the word of the Lord is the truth, and the messengers of the Lord are the truthful, and the believer who trusts the word is the believer whose trust the truth confirms, and the assurance of the angels is the comfort, the declaration that the decree of the Lord is the truth and the warning is the honest, and the believer who reads the assurance is the believer whose faith the truth strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the word: the messengers came with the truth, and the believer who trusts the word is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the truthfulness of the messengers is the assurance that the believer holds. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the truth that the believer trusts.",
  "65": "“So travel with your family in the dark of night, and follow ˹closely˺ behind them. Do not let any of you look back, and go where you are commanded.”” records the command of the departure, and the commentators note the three parts of the command. The first is the travel: “So travel with your family in the dark of night,” or “fa-asri bi-ahlika bi-qit‘in mina al-layli,” and the commentators note the command, for the angels commanded Lot to travel with the family in the night, and the night is the time of the departure, and the travel is the obedience, and the verse records the command as the instruction of the salvation. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the night is the cover, and the travel is the escape, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verses qualify. The second is the order: “and follow closely behind them. Do not let any of you look back,” or “wattabi‘ adbarahum wa la yaltafit minkum ahadun,” and the commentators note the order, for Lot was to follow behind the family, and the looking back was forbidden, and the prohibition is the teaching, for the looking back is the attachment to the world, and the believer must depart without the glance, and the verse records the order as the discipline of the salvation. The third is the destination: “and go where you are commanded,” or “wamdoo haythu tu’maroon,” and the commentators note the destination, for the family was to go where the angels commanded, and the destination is the safety, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the salvation required.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the departure, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the departure from the sin is the night, and the looking back is the attachment, and the believer who departs without the glance is the believer whose salvation the Lord completes, and the command of the angels is the model, the instruction of the departure that the believer must follow, and the destination that the Lord commands is the safety that the believer seeks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the departure: the travel at night and the prohibition of the look, and the believer who departs from the sin with the obedience is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the destination is the mercy that the believer attains. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the departure that the salvation requires.",
  "66": "“We revealed to him this decree: “Those ˹sinners˺ will be uprooted in the morning.”” records the revelation of the decree, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the revelation: “We revealed to him this decree: ‘Those sinners will be uprooted in the morning,’” or “wa qadayna ilayhi thalika al-amra anna dabira ha’ula’i maqtoo‘un musbiheen,” and the commentators note the revelation, for the Lord revealed to Lot the decree, and the decree is the uprooting, the “qat‘ ad-dabir,” the severing of the root, and the morning is the time, and the verse is the record of the revelation, the announcement of the destruction, and the commentators note the significance, for the decree is the warning, and the warning is the mercy, the knowledge that the morning would bring the ruin, and the verse is the record of the revelation, the decree that the salvation and the destruction would fulfill. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the uprooting is the destruction, and the morning is the hour, and the verse is the record of the revelation, the decree that the following verses apply.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the decree, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the decree of the Lord is the certain, and the one whom the Lord warns is the one whom the warning prepares, and the believer who knows the decree is the believer whose preparation the Lord guides, and the revelation of the decree is the mercy, the knowledge that the believer needs for the departure, and the believer who reads the revelation is the believer whose certainty the decree strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the decree: the uprooting was revealed, and the believer who trusts the warning is the believer whose preparation the Lord blesses, and the morning is the hour that the believer awaits with the trust. The verse is the record of the revelation, and the teaching of the decree that the believer trusts.",
  "67": "“And there came the men of the city, rejoicing.”” records the arrival of the men, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the arrival: “And there came the men of the city, rejoicing,” or “wa ja’a ahlu al-madeenati yastabshiroon,” and the commentators note the arrival, for the men of the city came to the house of Lot, and the word “yastabshiroon” — rejoicing — is the description, the eagerness of the men, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the prelude of the confrontation, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejoicing of the men is the measure of the corruption, the eagerness for the sin, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the rush that the following verses describe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the men came rejoicing, and the rejoicing is the shamelessness, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the prelude of the demand.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the shamelessness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the rejoicing in the sin is the measure of the corruption, and the one who is eager for the sin is the one whose heart the sin has mastered, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose joy the sin does not seize, and the rejoicing of the men is the warning, the description of the shamelessness that the punishment answers, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose fear the shamelessness sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the corruption of the joy: the men rejoiced in the sin, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose joy the Lord directs to the good, and the rejoicing is the prelude of the destruction. The verse is the record of the arrival, and the teaching of the shamelessness that the sin produces.",
  "68": "“Lot pleaded, “Indeed, these are my guests, so do not embarrass me.”” records the plea of Lot, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The first is the declaration: “Indeed, these are my guests,” or “qala inna ha’ula’i dayfee,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Lot declared that the angels were his guests, and the declaration is the appeal, the claim of the protection, and the verse records the declaration as the first defense of the guests. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the guest is the protected, and the declaration is the claim, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the defense that the following verse completes. The second part is the plea: “so do not embarrass me,” or “fa-la tafdahoon,” and the commentators note the plea, for Lot asked the men not to disgrace him, and the plea is the appeal to the honour, the seeking of the respect, and the verse is the record of the plea, the defense of the host, and the commentators note the significance, for the plea of Lot is the measure of the isolation, the prophet who stood alone against the crowd, and the verse is the record of the plea, the defense that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the defence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honour. The believer is taught that the defence of the guest is the duty of the host, and the one who protects the guest is the one whose honour the Lord values, and the believer who defends the wronged is the believer whose defence the Lord records, and the plea of Lot is the model, the defence of the guest that the host must make, and the believer who is threatened for the defence is the believer whose protection the Lord supplies. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the host: Lot defended the guests, and the believer who defends the wronged is the believer whose honour the Lord preserves, and the plea of Lot is the prelude of the deliverance. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the defence that the honour demands.",
  "69": "“Fear Allah and do not humiliate me.”” completes the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse completes the plea: “Fear Allah and do not humiliate me,” or “wattaqoo Allaha wa la tukhzoon,” and the commentators note the two commands, the fear of the Lord and the sparing of the humiliation, and the verse is the completion, the appeal of Lot to the fear and the honour, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear of the Lord is the appeal that the people had abandoned, and the sparing of the humiliation is the appeal to the dignity, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plea that the following verse answers with the rejection. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fear is the command, and the humiliation is the harm, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plea that the shamelessness refused.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own appeal. The believer is taught that the appeal to the fear of the Lord is the appeal of the believer, and the one who invokes the fear is the one whose appeal the heedless refuse, and the believer who appeals to the honour is the believer whose honour the Lord preserves, and the plea of Lot is the model, the appeal that the corruption rejected, and the believer who is rejected for the appeal is the believer whose vindication the Lord grants. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the appeal of the pure: Lot invoked the fear and the honour, and the believer who appeals to the truth is the believer whose appeal the Lord values, and the rejection of the plea is the measure of the corruption. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the appeal that the corruption refuses.",
  "70": "“They responded, “Have we not forbidden you from protecting anyone?”” records the reply of the people, and the commentators note the two elements of the reply. The verse records the reply: “Have we not forbidden you from protecting anyone?” or “qaloo awalam nanhaka ‘an al-‘alameen,” and the commentators note the reply, for the people declared that they had forbidden Lot from protecting the guests, and the reply is the shamelessness, the declaration that the protection of the people was the forbidden, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession of the corruption, and the commentators note the significance, for the reply reveals that the people had forbidden Lot from the good, and the forbidding of the good is the measure of the corruption, and the verse is the record of the reply, the shamelessness that the punishment answers. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the forbidding of the protection is the corruption, and the reply is the confession, and the verse is the record of the reply, the declaration that the following verses answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the shamelessness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the forbidding of the good is the corruption, and the one who forbids the protection is the one whose heart the sin has mastered, and the believer who commands the good is the believer whose command the Lord values, and the reply of the people is the warning, the description of the shamelessness that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose fear the shamelessness sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the corruption of the forbidding: the people forbade the protection, and the believer who commands the good is the believer whose command the Lord blesses, and the reply is the prelude of the destruction. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the shamelessness that the sin produces.",
  "71": "“He said, “O my people! Here are my daughters ˹so marry them˺ if you wish to do so.”” records the offer of Lot, and the commentators note the two parts of the offer. The verse records the offer: “O my people! Here are my daughters so marry them if you wish to do so,” or “qala ha’ula’i banatee in kuntum fa‘ileen,” and the commentators note the offer, for Lot offered the daughters in the lawful marriage, and the offer is the diversion, the attempt to turn the people to the lawful, and the verse is the record of the offer, the plea that the following verse answers with the description of the blindness, and the commentators note the significance, for the offer of the daughters is the last plea of the prophet, the attempt to save the people from the sin, and the verse is the record of the offer, the mercy that the corruption refused. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the offer is the diversion, and the diversion is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the offer, the plea that the blindness rejected.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the offer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the offer of the lawful is the mercy of the caller, and the one who diverts the sin to the lawful is the one whose mercy the Lord values, and the believer who calls the sinner to the lawful is the believer whose call the Lord records, and the offer of Lot is the model, the plea of the prophet for the people, and the believer who is rejected for the offer is the believer whose mercy the Lord will vindicate. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the caller: Lot offered the lawful, and the believer who diverts the sin is the believer whose mercy the Lord values, and the offer is the prelude of the judgment. The verse is the record of the offer, and the teaching of the mercy that the caller offers.",
  "72": "“By your life ˹O Prophet˺, they certainly wandered blindly, intoxicated ˹by lust˺.”” records the oath and the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the oath: “By your life O Prophet,” or “la-‘amruka,” and the commentators note the oath, for the Lord swore by the life of the Prophet, and the oath is the honour, the declaration that the life of the Prophet is the thing that the Lord swears by, and the commentators note the significance, for the oath is the unique honour, and the verse is the record of the oath, the honour that the Prophet received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oath by the life of the Prophet is the elevation, and the verse is the record of the oath, the honour that the following description confirms. The second part is the description: “they certainly wandered blindly, intoxicated by lust,” or “innahum la-fee sakratihim ya‘mahoon,” and the commentators note the description, for the people wandered in the blindness, the “sakrah,” the intoxication, the “‘amah,” the blind wandering, and the description is the verdict, the declaration that the people were the drunkards of the lust, and the verse is the record of the description, the verdict that the punishment answered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own clarity. The believer is taught that the sin is the intoxication, and the one who is drunk with the lust is the one who wanders blindly, and the believer who guards the clarity is the believer whose sight the Lord preserves, and the description of the people is the warning, the declaration that the lust blinds, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose fear the blindness sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the blindness of the sin: the people wandered in the intoxication, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose sight the Lord keeps clear, and the description is the prelude of the destruction. The verse is the record of the oath and the description, and the teaching of the blindness that the lust produces.",
  "73": "“So the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook them at sunrise.”” records the punishment, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the punishment: “So the mighty blast overtook them at sunrise,” or “fa-akhathathumu as-sayhatu mushriqeen,” and the commentators note the “sayhah,” the blast, and the “mushriqeen,” the sunrise, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the blast that struck the people at the dawn, and the commentators note the significance, for the punishment came at the sunrise, the time that the angels had named, and the timing is the certainty of the decree, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the first of the two instruments that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the blast is the punishment, and the sunrise is the hour, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the blast that the decree had appointed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment of the sin is the certain, and the one who persists in the sin is the one whom the blast overtakes, and the believer who fears the punishment is the believer who avoids the sin, and the timing of the sunrise is the teaching, the declaration that the hour of the judgment is the hour that the heedless do not expect, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose fear the certainty sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the judgment: the blast came at the sunrise, and the believer who avoids the sin is the believer whom the blast spares, and the timing is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the fear that the certainty produces.",
  "74": "“And We turned the cities ˹of Sodom and Gomorrah˺ upside down and rained upon them stones of baked clay.”” completes the punishment, and the commentators note the two instruments of the verse. The verse completes the punishment: “And We turned the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah upside down and rained upon them stones of baked clay,” or “fa-ja‘alna ‘aliyaha safilaha wa amtarna ‘alayhim hijaratan min sijjeel,” and the commentators note the two instruments, the overturning, the “‘aliyaha safilaha,” the high made low, and the raining of the stones, the “hijarah min sijjeel,” the stones of the baked clay, and the verse is the record of the completion, the two punishments that the sin earned, and the commentators note the significance, for the overturning is the inversion that the sin of the inversion deserved, and the stones are the rain that the corruption invited, and the verse is the record of the completion, the punishment that the following verse reflects upon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the overturning is the inversion, and the stones are the rain, and the verse is the record of the completion, the two instruments of the destruction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inversion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the sin of the inversion is the sin that the inversion answers, and the one who inverts the order of the Lord is the one whose order the Lord inverts, and the believer who guards the order is the believer whose order the Lord preserves, and the two instruments are the warning, the declaration that the corruption meets the complete ruin, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose fear the completeness sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the punishment: the cities were overturned and stoned, and the believer who guards the order is the believer whom the inversion spares, and the punishment is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the inversion that the sin invites.",
  "75": "“Surely in this are signs for those who contemplate.”” records the reflection, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the reflection: “Surely in this are signs for those who contemplate,” or “inna fee thalika la-ayatin lil-mutawassimeen,” and the commentators note the “mutawassimeen,” the contemplators, the ones who read the signs, and the verse is the declaration, the assurance that the punishment of the people of Lot is the sign, and the sign is for the ones who contemplate, and the commentators note the significance, for the contemplation is the reading, and the reading is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the invitation to the contemplation that the believer must make. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the signs are the lessons, and the contemplators are the ones who extract, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the invitation that the following verse repeats.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the contemplation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the punishment of the past is the sign, and the one who contemplates the sign is the one who learns the lesson, and the believer who reflects upon the histories is the believer whose fear the reflection sharpens, and the signs of the verse are the invitation, the call to the contemplation that the believer must answer, and the believer who reads the histories with the contemplation is the believer whose heart the histories guide. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the contemplation: the signs are for the contemplators, and the believer who reflects is the believer who extracts, and the reflection is the key of the lesson. The verse is the record of the reflection, and the teaching of the contemplation that the signs invite.",
  "76": "“Their ruins still lie along a known route.”” records the location, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the location: “Their ruins still lie along a known route,” or “wa innaha la-bi-sabeelin muqeem,” and the commentators note the “sabeel muqeem,” the established route, the known road, and the verse is the declaration, the assurance that the ruins of the people of Lot lie along the road that the travellers pass, and the commentators note the significance, for the ruins are the witness, the visible sign that the travellers see, and the verse is the record of the location, the proof that the punishment was the real, and the visible ruins are the lesson that the passing heedless ignore. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruins are the sign, and the route is the road, and the verse is the record of the location, the witness that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the witness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sight. The believer is taught that the ruins of the past are the witness, and the one who passes the ruins is the one whom the witness addresses, and the believer who sees the ruins with the contemplation is the believer whose lesson the ruins teach, and the known route is the teaching, the declaration that the sign is the open, and the believer who reads the location is the believer whose fear the visibility sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the openness of the sign: the ruins are on the route, and the believer who sees them is the believer who learns, and the visibility is the mercy that the heedless neglect. The verse is the record of the location, and the teaching of the witness that the ruins offer.",
  "77": "“Surely in this is a sign for those who believe.”” repeats the reflection, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse repeats the reflection: “Surely in this is a sign for those who believe,” or “inna fee thalika la-ayatan lil-mu’mineen,” and the commentators note the “mu’mineen,” the believers, and the verse is the declaration, the assurance that the sign of the ruins is for the believers, and the commentators note the significance, for the two verses of the reflection address the two groups, the contemplators and the believers, and the two groups are the ones who receive the sign, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the repetition that the following verse connects to the next account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is for the believers, and the believers are the ones who read, and the verse is the record of the reflection, the repetition that the following accounts continue.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are for the believers, and the one who believes is the one whom the signs guide, and the believer who reads the histories with the faith is the believer whose faith the histories strengthen, and the sign of the ruins is the promise, the declaration that the believer is the one who receives the lesson, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose sight the sign opens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the faith: the signs are for the believers, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whom the signs guide, and the reflection is the key that the faith turns. The verse is the record of the reflection, and the teaching of the sign that the faith receives.",
  "78": "“And the residents of the Forest were truly wrongdoers,”” introduces the next community, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse introduces the community: “And the residents of the Forest were truly wrongdoers,” or “wa in kana ashabu al-aykati la-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the “ashab al-aykah,” the residents of the Forest, the people of Shu‘ayb, the Midian, and the “zalimeen,” the wrongdoers, and the verse is the introduction, the declaration of the sin of the people of the Forest, and the commentators note the significance, for the people of the Forest are the people of Shu‘ayb, and their sin was the corruption of the measure, and the verse is the record of the introduction, the step that the following verse completes with the punishment. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the residents of the Forest are the Midian, and the wrong is the corruption, and the verse is the record of the introduction, the declaration of the sin.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sin, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own warning. The believer is taught that the wrong of the measure is the sin that the punishment answers, and the one who corrupts the scale is the one whose scale the Lord weighs, and the believer who deals with the justice is the believer whose dealing the Lord blesses, and the wrong of the people of the Forest is the warning, the declaration that the corruption of the scale is the sin, and the believer who reads the introduction is the believer whose fear the sin sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the wrong: the people of the Forest were the wrongdoers, and the believer who guards the scale is the believer whom the Lord blesses, and the wrong is the prelude of the punishment. The verse is the record of the introduction, and the teaching of the sin that the corruption carries.",
  "79": "“so We inflicted punishment upon them. The ruins of both nations still lie on a well-known road.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the punishment: “so We inflicted punishment upon them,” or “fantaqamna minhum,” and the commentators note the punishment, for the Lord took the vengeance upon the people of the Forest, and the punishment is the answer to the wrong, and the verse records the punishment as the completion of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the vengeance is the justice, and the justice is the recompense, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the answer that the wrong earned. The second part is the location: “The ruins of both nations still lie on a well-known road,” or “wa innahuma la-bi-imamin mubeen,” and the commentators note the “imam mubeen,” the clear road, the manifest path, and the verse is the declaration, the assurance that the ruins of the two nations — the people of Lot and the people of the Forest — lie along the clear road, and the commentators note the significance, for the two ruins are the two witnesses, the visible signs that the travellers pass, and the verse is the record of the location, the proof that the punishments were the real.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the witness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sight. The believer is taught that the ruins of the past are the witnesses, and the two nations are the two signs, and the believer who sees the ruins with the contemplation is the believer whose lesson the ruins teach, and the clear road is the teaching, the declaration that the signs are the open, and the believer who reads the location is the believer whose fear the visibility sharpens, and the two witnesses are the proof that the law of the Lord is the constant. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the law: the two nations and the two ruins, and the believer who reads the histories is the believer who learns the single law, and the clear road is the mercy that the heedless pass by. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the witness that the ruins offer.",
  "80": "“Indeed, the residents of the Stone Valley also denied the messengers.”” introduces the Thamud, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse introduces the community: “Indeed, the residents of the Stone Valley also denied the messengers,” or “wa laqad kaththaba ashabu al-hijri al-mursaleen,” and the commentators note the “ashab al-hijr,” the residents of the Stone Valley, the Thamud, and the denial of the messengers, and the verse is the introduction, the declaration of the sin of the Thamud, and the commentators note the significance, for the denial of the one messenger is the denial of the line, and the verse is the record of the introduction, the step that the following verse develops. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the residents of the Stone Valley are the Thamud, and the denial is the sin, and the verse is the record of the introduction, the declaration of the rejection.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the denial, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the denial of the messenger is the denial of the line, and the one who rejects the messenger is the one who rejects the message, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord records, and the denial of the Thamud is the warning, the declaration that the rejection is the sin, and the believer who reads the introduction is the believer whose faith the denial sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the denial: the Thamud denied the messengers, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whom the Lord honours, and the denial is the prelude of the punishment. The verse is the record of the introduction, and the teaching of the denial that the rejection carries.",
  "81": "“We gave them Our signs, but they turned away from them.”” records the rejection of the signs, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the rejection: “We gave them Our signs, but they turned away from them,” or “wa ataynahum ayatina fa-kanoo ‘anha mu‘rideen,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the giving of the signs and the turning away, and the verse is the declaration, the teaching that the Thamud received the signs and rejected them, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection of the signs after the giving is the gravest of the sins, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the step that the following verse develops. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the signs were the she-camel and the proofs, and the turning away is the refusal, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the refusal that the punishment answered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own acceptance. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the gifts, and the one who turns from the gift is the one whose rejection the Lord judges, and the believer who accepts the signs is the believer whose acceptance the Lord rewards, and the rejection of the Thamud is the warning, the declaration that the turning away is the sin, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose acceptance the warning strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the signs: the Thamud turned away, and the believer who accepts the signs is the believer whom the Lord guides, and the rejection is the prelude of the destruction. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the acceptance that the signs demand.",
  "82": "“They carved their homes in the mountains, feeling secure.”” records the security of the Thamud, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the security: “They carved their homes in the mountains, feeling secure,” or “wa kanoo yanhitoona mina al-jibali buyootan amineen,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the carving of the homes and the security, and the verse is the declaration, the teaching that the Thamud trusted the mountains, and the commentators note the significance, for the security of the homes is the illusion, and the illusion is the thing that the punishment shattered, and the verse is the record of the security, the trust that the following verse answers with the blast. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the homes were the pride, and the security was the illusion, and the verse is the record of the security, the trust that the punishment broke.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the security, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the security of the world is the illusion, and the one who trusts the homes is the one whose trust the decree shatters, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose security the Lord grants, and the security of the Thamud is the warning, the declaration that the refuge of the world is the refuge that fails, and the believer who reads the security is the believer whose trust the warning corrects. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fragility of the world: the Thamud trusted the mountains, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose refuge the decree spares, and the security is the prelude of the destruction. The verse is the record of the security, and the teaching of the trust that the world cannot secure.",
  "83": "“But the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook them in the morning,”” records the punishment of the Thamud, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the punishment: “But the mighty blast overtook them in the morning,” or “fa-akhathathumu as-sayhatu musbiheen,” and the commentators note the “sayhah,” the blast, and the “musbiheen,” the morning, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the blast that struck the Thamud at the dawn, and the commentators note the significance, for the punishment came in the morning, the time of the security, and the timing is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment comes when the heedless feel the secure, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the first half that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the blast is the punishment, and the morning is the hour, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the blast that the security did not avert.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment comes at the hour of the security, and the one who trusts the world is the one whom the blast surprises, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord honours, and the timing of the morning is the warning, the declaration that the heedlessness of the dawn is the heedlessness that the judgment answers, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose fear the certainty sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the surprise of the judgment: the blast came in the morning, and the believer who lives in the fear is the believer whom the judgment finds ready, and the timing is the warning that the believer must heed. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the fear that the certainty produces.",
  "84": "“and all they achieved was of no help to them.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the account: “and all they achieved was of no help to them,” or “fa-ma aghna ‘anhum ma kanoo yaksiboon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the achievements of the Thamud — the carved homes and the wealth — availed them nothing against the blast, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the works of the world do not shield against the decree, and the commentators note the significance, for the verse is the verdict upon the security, the declaration that the refuge of the achievement is the refuge that fails, and the verse is the record of the completion, the lesson that the following chapters repeat. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the achievements are the world, and the world is the thing that the decree does not respect, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the believer must not trust the works.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the works, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the works of the world are the refuge that fails, and the one who trusts the achievement is the one whose achievement the decree nullifies, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose refuge the Lord supplies, and the verdict of the verse is the warning, the declaration that the world is the thing that the decree overcomes, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the warning corrects. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the vanity of the world: the achievements availed nothing, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose refuge the Lord preserves, and the works of the Thamud are the witness of the vanity. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the world cannot supply.",
  "85": "\"We have not created the heavens and the earth and everything in between except for a purpose.\" turns from three destroyed communities to the reason any of it matters.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates creation with truth, meaning for a genuine end rather than as play or accident. The Quran presses this claim repeatedly, asking elsewhere whether people thought they were created purposelessly and would not be returned.\n\nPlacing it here draws the accounts together. The destructions just described were not random catastrophes in a meaningless universe. They occurred within an arrangement that has a purpose, and the purpose is what makes accountability possible.\n\n\"And the Hour is certain to come\" states the consequence. If creation has a purpose, it has an end at which the purpose is fulfilled.\n\n\"So forgive graciously.\" is the instruction that follows, and its placement is genuinely surprising.\n\nAfter three accounts of communities destroyed for rejecting their messengers, addressed to a Prophet whose own people were mocking him and calling him insane, the instruction given is to forgive.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates a beautiful overlooking, pardoning without reproach or bitterness. Commentators explained it as forgiving in a manner that involves no rebuke afterwards, letting something go completely rather than holding it in reserve.\n\nThe logic connecting the two halves of the verse is worth drawing out. Because the Hour is certain, and because judgement belongs to Allah, the Prophet is released from the need to settle accounts himself. Certainty about the outcome is precisely what makes gracious pardon possible.\n\nA person who believes nothing will ever be put right must seek redress personally. Someone certain that everything will be addressed can afford to let insults pass.\n\nThis same disposition appeared throughout the account of Joseph, who forgave brothers who had destroyed his childhood and prayed for their forgiveness.",
  "86": "\"Surely your Lord is the Master Creator, All-Knowing.\" gives the two names that ground everything stated in the previous verse.\n\nThe first indicates the One who creates continuously and completely, the Creator in the fullest sense. Its form in Arabic conveys constancy: not one who created once and stopped, but one whose creating is ongoing.\n\nThat name answers the first half of the previous verse, which stated that the heavens and the earth were created for a purpose. A creator who fashioned something and abandoned it might leave a universe with no follow-through. This name describes the opposite.\n\nThe second name indicates complete knowledge. It answers the second half of the previous verse, concerning the certainty of the Hour, since a reckoning is only meaningful if everything is known.\n\nTogether the two names cover the whole arrangement. He made it, and He knows it, which means the purpose will be fulfilled and the accounting will be accurate.\n\nThere is a further connection worth noticing. The verse before it instructed the Prophet to forgive graciously, and this verse tells him who his Lord is.\n\nForgiving requires confidence in someone else handling the matter. A person who overlooks an injury is not saying the injury did not occur; he is declining to pursue it himself. That is only reasonable if someone with complete knowledge is keeping the record.\n\nThe word \"your Lord\" is worth noticing too. The Arabic \"Rabb\" indicates the One who owns, sustains, nurtures, and cares for. In a chapter addressed to a Prophet under sustained mockery, the reminder is personal rather than abstract.\n\nHe is being told that the One who made everything and knows everything is his own Lord, and that this relationship is the basis on which he can afford to let insults pass without carrying them.\n\nThe following verse names a specific gift given to him, which functions as evidence of exactly this care.",
  "87": "\"We have certainly granted you the seven often-repeated verses and the great Quran.\" names two gifts, and the first has been the subject of considerable discussion.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates seven of the repeated ones. The most widely accepted explanation, supported by an authentic hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, is that this refers to Surah al-Fatihah, the opening chapter of seven verses. The Prophet told a companion that al-Fatihah is the seven oft-repeated verses and the great Quran that he was given.\n\nSome early commentators offered other views, including that it refers to seven long chapters of the Quran. The Prophet's own explanation settles the matter for most scholars.\n\nThe description of these verses as often-repeated is accurate in a way that has only become more obvious with time. Al-Fatihah is recited in every unit of every prayer, which means a Muslim performing the five daily prayers recites it at least seventeen times a day, and it has been recited that way by millions of people for fourteen centuries.\n\nThe second gift is the Quran itself, described as great.\n\nThe placement of this verse is what gives it force. The Prophet was being mocked, his people were demanding angels, and he was watching wealthy opponents enjoy comfort he did not share. This verse tells him what he actually has.\n\nThe next verse makes the comparison explicit by instructing him not to look longingly at what others have been given.\n\nThe granting is also communal. The Prophet received these verses, and through him every Muslim received them; no other passage of the Quran is placed on the tongue of every worshipper in every prayer, whatever a person's learning, age, or native tongue. A gift given to one man turned out to be the inheritance of everyone who followed him.\n\nThere is something in this arrangement that applies well beyond the original situation. A person who feels the lack of what others possess is often carrying something considerably more valuable without registering it, and the remedy the Quran applies here is not to argue about the value of wealth but to name what he already holds.",
  "88": "\"Do not let your eyes crave the ˹fleeting˺ pleasures We have provided for some of the disbelievers\" addresses a temptation that follows naturally from the previous verse.\n\nThe Prophet had just been reminded of what he was given: the seven repeated verses and the great Quran. Now he is told not to stretch his eyes toward what others were given instead.\n\nThe Arabic indicates extending the gaze, looking with longing at something one does not have. The image is precise. It describes the eye lingering rather than passing over.\n\nWhat the disbelievers had been given was material comfort. The Meccan leaders were wealthy, and the small Muslim community was largely poor, with many of its members enslaved or without protection.\n\nNote that the verse does not deny they had been given something real. It says We have provided it, which is an honest acknowledgement.\n\nWhat it disputes is the value of the comparison. The Quran elsewhere describes such provision as a temporary enjoyment, and Surah Ta-Ha uses almost identical language, instructing the Prophet not to extend his eyes toward what has been given as the splendour of worldly life, adding that the provision of his Lord is better and more lasting.\n\n\"Nor grieve for them.\" addresses a different reaction, and its inclusion is worth noticing.\n\nThe Prophet's distress was not envy. Commentators have generally understood this grief as sorrow over their refusal to believe, which several verses in the Quran describe him experiencing intensely.\n\n\"And be gracious to the believers.\" gives the positive instruction, and the Arabic image is lowering one's wing, the way a bird lowers its wing over its young. It indicates gentleness, protection, and humility toward those under one's care.\n\nThe three instructions together redirect attention completely: away from what others have, away from grief over their choices, and toward the people who are actually present.",
  "89": "\"\"And say, \"I am truly sent with a clear warning\"—\" is the declaration the Prophet is instructed to make, and it follows a series of instructions about where his attention should rest.\n\nHaving been told not to look longingly at what others possess, not to grieve over their refusal, and to be gentle with the believers, he is now told what to announce about himself.\n\nThe statement is short and contains no argument. He is not told to debate, defend himself against the accusation of insanity recorded earlier in this chapter, or produce the angels they had demanded. He states his role.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a warner who makes things clear, and commentators have noted that the description covers both the clarity of the warning and the clarity of the one delivering it. There is nothing ambiguous about either.\n\nThat word for clarity has appeared throughout this chapter. It opened by describing the Book as the clear Quran, and the flare pursuing eavesdroppers was described as clearly visible.\n\nBeing a warner is a role the Quran assigns to the Prophet repeatedly, and it carries a specific limit that the surrounding chapters have made explicit. A warner delivers; he does not compel, guarantee results, or manufacture belief.\n\nThere is relief in that limitation for anyone carrying a message. The Prophet's success was never measured by how many accepted, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated plainly that his duty was only to deliver while the reckoning belongs to Allah.\n\nThe Prophet described his own position with an image recorded in al-Bukhari and Muslim, comparing himself to a man who has seen an approaching army and runs to warn his people, calling out to them because he saw what they could not.\n\nThat image captures why the warning is delivered with urgency rather than detachment, and why the warner keeps calling even when the people he is calling are laughing at him.",
  "90": "\"˹A warning˺ similar to what We sent to those who divided ˹the Scriptures˺,\" connects the Prophet's warning to something delivered before, and the group named requires explanation.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates those who divided or apportioned something. Commentators have offered several explanations, and they are worth presenting honestly.\n\nMany understood it as referring to earlier communities of the People of the Book who divided their scripture, accepting some of it and rejecting the rest, which fits the following verse exactly.\n\nOthers explained it as a group of Meccans who divided the roads into the city during the pilgrimage season, stationing themselves on different routes to intercept arriving travellers and warn them against the Prophet. Reports of this activity exist in the historical literature, and it was an organised campaign.\n\nBoth readings describe a form of dividing, and both fit the context. The first concerns dividing a message; the second concerns dividing access to one.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is that the Prophet's warning belongs to a series. The same warning was sent before, and the people who received it dealt with it in a particular way.\n\nThat connection serves the chapter's purpose. The Prophet was facing something with precedent, and knowing this changes how the difficulty is experienced.\n\nThere is also an implicit caution for the Muslim community in this verse. If earlier communities were warned about dividing their scripture, the warning is available to anyone holding a scripture, and the Quran repeatedly notes that earlier peoples divided only after clear knowledge came to them.\n\nThe two readings converge in any case. Whether a community splits its scripture into accepted and rejected portions, or splits the roads so that travellers hear only one side, the method is identical: control what people encounter, and you shape what they believe they know. The warning stands against both, and the next verse shows the same habit at work on the revelation itself.\n\nThe following verse describes the dividing precisely, and the description turns out to be uncomfortably recognisable in every religious community, including those who consider themselves faithful to a text.",
  "91": "\"Who ˹now˺ accept parts of the Quran, rejecting others.\" describes what the dividing actually consisted of.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making something into portions or fragments. Commentators have explained the reference in more than one way, and the range is worth noting. Many understood it as accepting some of the revelation and refusing the rest. Others explained it as fragmenting the Book by calling it poetry, magic, or ancient tales, dividing it up with various dismissive labels.\n\nBoth descriptions identify the same underlying behaviour: treating a message as something to be sorted rather than received.\n\nSelective acceptance is a recognisable habit, and the chapter is right to name it. A person encounters a body of guidance and finds that some of it suits him. Those parts are embraced enthusiastically, quoted, and defended. The parts that conflict with his interests, culture, or desires are quietly set aside, explained away, or treated as less important.\n\nWhat makes this so difficult to detect is that the person doing it genuinely believes in the source. He is not rejecting the Book; he is arranging it.\n\nThe Quran addresses this elsewhere with unusual severity. Surah al-Baqarah asks a community whether they believe in part of the Scripture and disbelieve in another part, and states that the recompense for such conduct is disgrace in worldly life and severe punishment on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nThe logic behind that severity is straightforward. If a person accepts only what he already agrees with, the source has no authority over him. He is following his own judgement and using revelation to decorate it.\n\nThis is not a warning aimed only at earlier communities. Every religious tradition, including Muslims, produces people who hold their scripture in high honour and read it selectively.\n\nThe verses that follow state what happens to such people, and the answer applies to everyone without distinction.",
  "92": "\"So by your Lord! We will certainly question them all\" is an oath followed by a promise, and the combination gives it considerable force.\n\nAllah swears by Himself, described here as the Prophet's Lord, and the Arabic construction that follows is emphatic in multiple ways: a particle of emphasis and an emphatic verb ending, both reinforcing the certainty of the questioning.\n\nOaths in the Quran are used when something needs to be settled beyond dispute, and what is being settled here is that everyone will be asked.\n\nThe phrase \"all of them\" is the part that carries the weight. Not the leaders only, not the notorious, not those who committed obvious crimes. Every one of them.\n\nThat scope is worth pausing over, because people naturally assume categories of exemption. The follower assumes his leader carries the responsibility, and Surah Ibrahim recorded exactly that assumption collapsing in the exchange between the weak and the arrogant. The obscure person assumes he is too insignificant to be examined.\n\nThe verse removes both assumptions with a single word.\n\nThe use of \"your Lord\" in the oath is also worth noticing. In a chapter addressed to a Prophet whose people were dividing his message, accepting parts and rejecting others, the oath is sworn by the One who is his Lord specifically.\n\nThere is reassurance in that framing as well as warning. The people who fragmented the revelation and mocked the one who brought it are going to be asked about it, and the questioning is guaranteed by an oath.\n\nWhat they will be questioned about is stated in the following verse, and the answer turns out to be broader than the specific offence just described. The examination is not limited to how they treated the Book, which means the guarantee applies to everyone reading it as much as to those it originally addressed.",
  "93": "\"About what they used to do.\" completes the oath begun in the previous verse, and it defines the subject of the questioning.\n\nThe Arabic indicates what they were doing habitually, using a construction that conveys ongoing, repeated action rather than isolated incidents. The examination concerns the pattern of a life rather than a list of dramatic moments.\n\nThat is worth dwelling on. People tend to imagine judgement as an examination of a few significant decisions, the crises and turning points where character was visibly tested. The wording here points somewhere else: at what a person was in the habit of doing.\n\nHabits are made of small things repeated. How a person spoke to those who could do nothing for him, whether he prayed when nobody expected it, what he did with an hour nobody was accounting for. None of these register as events, and together they constitute most of a life.\n\nThe scope of the questioning is also unrestricted. The previous verses described people dividing the revelation, accepting parts and rejecting others, and this verse does not limit the examination to that offence.\n\nThe Prophet described the shape of this questioning in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi, saying that the feet of a servant will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about four things: his life and how he spent it, his knowledge and what he did with it, his wealth, where he earned it and how he spent it, and his body and how he used it.\n\nEvery item on that list concerns ordinary conduct rather than exceptional moments.\n\nThere is a practical use for this verse that costs nothing to apply. A person can examine what he actually does with his ordinary days, since that is precisely what the question will be about, and adjusting a habit is considerably easier than reversing a lifetime.",
  "94": "\"So proclaim what you have been commanded, and turn away from the polytheists.\" gives the Prophet a direct instruction, and commentators have identified this as a significant moment in the Meccan period.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates striking through or splitting, and it conveys announcing something openly and forcefully rather than speaking quietly. Several early commentators reported that the Prophet had been calling to Islam privately for a period, and that this verse marked the beginning of open, public proclamation.\n\nWhether or not one accepts every detail of those reports, the instruction itself is clear. What he had been commanded was to be announced without concealment.\n\nThe content is specified precisely: what you have been commanded. Not his own views, not what would be well received, and not an adjusted version calculated to reduce hostility. The message as given.\n\nThat limitation protects a caller in both directions. He is not free to add, and he is not permitted to subtract.\n\n\"And turn away from the polytheists.\" is the second half, and it needs to be understood correctly.\n\nThe Arabic indicates disregarding them, paying no attention to their objections and mockery. It does not mean ceasing to convey the message to them, since the verse has just commanded open proclamation to everyone.\n\nWhat is being described is an attitude rather than a withdrawal. Announce the message publicly, and stop being affected by the reaction.\n\nThose two instructions belong together, and each is difficult without the other. Public proclamation invites public ridicule, and a person who remains sensitive to ridicule will soften what he says until nothing remains of it.\n\nThe chapter has already recorded what he was facing: being called insane, being challenged to produce angels, and watching his message fragmented into parts to be accepted or dismissed.\n\nThe assurance that makes this instruction possible follows immediately.",
  "95": "\"Surely We will be sufficient for you against the mockers,\" is the guarantee that accompanies the instruction to proclaim openly and disregard the opposition.\n\nThe Arabic indicates being enough for someone, taking care of a matter on his behalf so that he does not need to handle it himself.\n\nThe Prophet is not told to defend himself, answer the mockery, or organise protection. He is told that this particular problem has been taken over.\n\nThat is what makes the previous instruction possible. Announcing a message publicly in a hostile city, and then ignoring the reaction, requires knowing that someone else is dealing with the consequences.\n\nThe mockers are named specifically. This chapter opened with them calling him insane, and mockery has been the recurring weapon throughout: Noah laughed at while building, Hud accused of madness, Salih dismissed as a disappointment, and every messenger before him ridiculed, as this chapter stated plainly.\n\nHistorical reports mention a small group of Quraysh leaders who took the lead in this campaign, and several of them met unpleasant ends before the Prophet's migration. Commentators connect this verse with those events, and the details vary in strength across the reports.\n\nWhat is certain is the principle rather than any particular case. Allah undertook to handle the mockers, and the Prophet was freed from having to.\n\nThere is something in this worth extracting for anyone facing ridicule for their faith. The instinct is to answer, to prove the mockers wrong, to win the exchange. The verse offers a different arrangement: deliver the message, ignore the response, and leave that particular account to be settled elsewhere.\n\nThe guarantee also marks the limit of the Prophet's responsibility, and servants of the message still work inside that limit. Delivering was his task; outcomes were not. No messenger was ever charged with making people believe, and Allah's sufficiency against the mockers meant the account for mockery was never his to settle in the first place.\n\nThe following verse identifies who these mockers were in terms of their actual offence, which turns out to be considerably more serious than their treatment of one man.",
  "96": "\"Who set up ˹other˺ gods with Allah.\" identifies the mockers by their real offence rather than by their behaviour toward the Prophet.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making something a partner alongside Allah, treating another as a peer to Him.\n\nThat framing is significant. These people had been ridiculing a man in the streets of Mecca, and the verse describes their fundamental problem as something else entirely. Their mockery was a symptom.\n\nThere is a logic connecting the two. A person who assigns divine status to carved objects has already abandoned the standard by which claims are evaluated. Someone whose religion rests on inherited assumption rather than evidence will naturally treat a message based on evidence as absurd, since he has no category for it.\n\nSurah Yunus made this connection explicitly, describing them as following nothing but assumptions while assumptions can never take the place of truth.\n\n\"They will soon come to know.\" closes the verse with a phrase that carries considerable weight in its brevity.\n\nThe Arabic indicates knowledge that arrives through experience rather than through argument. They will not be convinced; they will find out.\n\nThe word for soon is doing quiet work here. From the standpoint of people who had been mocking for years without consequence, nothing seemed imminent. The chapter opened by describing exactly that attitude, telling them to eat, enjoy themselves, and be diverted by hope, because they would soon know.\n\nThat phrase now returns at the chapter's close, forming a frame around everything between: three destroyed communities, a guarded sky, the account of Iblis, and the instruction to proclaim openly.\n\nThe phrase 'with Allah' is the heart of the charge. The offence is not merely worshipping something other than Allah, which could be simple ignorance, but setting anything in His company, a claim about who deserves what belongs to Him alone. That is why the verse can name their crime without describing a single ritual: the arrangement itself, whoever the partners were, was the problem.\n\nFor the mockers, the promise was fulfilled within a few years. For the Prophet, it meant that the account he had been told to ignore was being kept accurately by someone else.",
  "97": "\"We certainly know that your heart is truly distressed by what they say.\" is an acknowledgement rather than an instruction, and its presence in the chapter is worth noticing.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a chest becoming constricted, tightening. It is a physical description of a familiar sensation: the tension that settles in the body under sustained pressure.\n\nThe Prophet is not told that he should not feel this. He is told that it is known.\n\nThat distinction matters a great deal. A person struggling under strain is often met with correction, told he should be stronger or should not let things affect him. The verse does none of that. It names the condition accurately and confirms that it has been seen.\n\nThere is a form of relief in being understood that no advice can provide, and the Quran offers it here before offering anything else.\n\nWhat was causing the distress is specified: what they say. Not physical danger, though there was plenty of that, but words. He was called insane, accused of fabrication, described as a poet and a soothsayer, and his message was fragmented into parts to be accepted or dismissed.\n\nWords wound in a way that is easy to underestimate, particularly for someone whose entire mission consisted of speech. Every insult aimed at his credibility was aimed at the thing he was carrying.\n\nSurah al-An'am offers a related consolation, telling him that it is not him they deny but the signs of Allah that the wrongdoers reject.\n\nThe knowledge named here cuts both ways in the kindest manner. Allah knew what was said and also what the saying did to the man who heard it, so no insult went unrecorded from either side. Taken with the promise of sufficiency in the previous verse, the arrangement is complete: the harm was fully seen, and the settling of it had been assigned.\n\nThe instruction that follows this acknowledgement is not to suppress the feeling or reason it away. What is prescribed in the next verse is something to do, and the remedy offered turns out to be worship rather than argument or self-discipline.",
  "98": "\"So glorify the praises of your Lord and be one of those who ˹always˺ pray,\" is the remedy prescribed for the distress just acknowledged, and it is worth examining why this remedy rather than another.\n\nThe Prophet was not told to argue better, avoid his critics, or reason himself out of the feeling. He was told to worship.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates declaring Allah free of every fault while praising Him. The two acts are joined: denying any deficiency and affirming every perfection. Muslims perform this constantly in the short formulas of remembrance recited after prayer and throughout the day.\n\nThere is a connection between this practice and the specific distress described. He was being told, day after day, that his message was false and that the One he worshipped had no exclusive claim. Glorification is the direct answer to that, and repeating it restores in the heart what the mockery attacks.\n\nBeing among those who prostrate is the second half, and it moves from words to posture.\n\nProstration is the position in which a person places his face on the ground, and the Prophet described it as the closest a servant comes to his Lord, in a hadith recorded by Muslim, encouraging abundant supplication in that state.\n\nFor a man whose dignity was under public assault, the prescribed response was to put his forehead on the floor before Allah. There is something in that which cuts through the entire logic of humiliation. A person whose standing rests on Allah cannot be diminished by people, and prostration is the physical enactment of that fact.\n\nIt is also worth noticing what prayer does practically. Distress isolates a person inside his own thoughts, and turning to worship interrupts the loop and directs attention outward and upward.\n\nThe Prophet's own practice reflected this. It is reported in the hadith literature that when a matter troubled him, he would hasten to prayer.",
  "99": "\"And worship your Lord until the inevitable comes your way.\" closes the chapter with a single instruction and a fixed endpoint.\n\nThe Arabic word rendered here as the inevitable indicates certainty, the thing about which there is no doubt. Commentators are agreed that it refers to death, and Ibn Abbas and others explained it in exactly those terms.\n\nDeath is described as the certainty because it is the one event nobody disputes. People argue about resurrection, about revelation, about the existence of a reckoning. Nobody argues about dying.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is the duration of worship: until that moment and not before. There is no stage of life at which a person graduates from it, no level of piety that permits retirement, and no achievement that closes the account early.\n\nThis has been misunderstood in a particular way that deserves correction. Some groups in Islamic history claimed that a person who reaches a certain spiritual state is relieved of religious obligations, reading the word for certainty as a station of knowledge rather than as death. Scholars rejected this firmly, and the reason is straightforward. The Prophet was the most certain of all human beings and worshipped until his final illness, praying while unable to stand.\n\nThere is also encouragement in the verse alongside the obligation. Worship continues until death, which means the door remains open until then. A person who has wasted decades still has the remainder, and Islamic teaching consistently affirms that repentance is accepted while life continues.\n\nThe chapter has arrived at a fitting close. It opened with people who would wish they had submitted, described their diversion by eating, enjoyment, and long hopes, and told them they would soon know.\n\nIt ends by telling the Prophet, and everyone reading, what to do with the interval: worship, until the certainty arrives."
};
